-- After this, edit the content in Training > Road Map > Edit content.

select public.academy__import('54f3e299-1f61-4ed2-9921-3d02160b72e6', $academy${"program":{"title":"Mighty Wash Academy","eyebrow":"Road Map to Success","headline":"Seven levels. One standard.","intro":"Training for every role at Mighty Wash, from the first day on the lot to running a region. Each level has lessons with the why behind each standard, a knowledge check, scenario practice, an assessment, and a sign-off.","why_program":"Every role at Mighty Wash is a step on one ladder, the Road Map to Success. Each level in this Academy teaches the standards for that role, explains why each one exists, lets you practice the decisions, checks that it stuck, and ends with a sign-off. Finish your level, then start the next one. The person who knows the next level's standards is the person who gets promoted into it.","why_scenarios":"Knowing the standard and applying it under pressure are different skills. Each scenario is a moment where the easy option and the standard pull in opposite directions. Making the decision here, with time to think, makes it faster and more automatic on the lot.","why_assessment":"The assessment confirms the standards made it from the page into memory, and gives you and your leader one shared record of that. The 80% pass mark is high on purpose: these are the standards your role is measured on, so knowing most of them is not enough.","why_signoff":"The sign-off turns the standards from an announcement into an agreement. It confirms you have completed the level, understand how your role is measured, and agree to hold the standard at your site. That is the same thing the Leadership Manual asks every leader to do with a new hire on day one.","draft_note":"Draft standards for this level, written to match the issued GM Standards and Leadership Manual. Pending review by Mighty Wash leadership; details may change."},"levels":[{"key":"l1","num":1,"title":"Quality Control / Passenger Side Prep","short":"QC / Passenger Prep","role":"Entry level. Every Mighty Wash career starts here. You prep the passenger side before the tunnel and inspect every car at the exit.","blurb":"Safety, prep technique, exit quality control, conduct on the lot, and site readiness.","why":"You are the first hands on the car and the last eyes on it. The tunnel does most of the work, but what it cannot reach and what it misses is yours. A car that leaves dirty blames the wash, not the equipment.","draft":true,"pass":80,"ack_formal":false,"ack_title":null,"ack_statement":"I have completed the Quality Control / Passenger Side Prep training. I know the safety rules, the prep standard, the exit quality check, the conduct rules, and the site readiness standard, and I will perform them on every shift.","courses":[{"title":"Quality Control and Passenger Side Prep","blurb":null,"note":null,"draft":false,"lessons":[{"key":"p1","title":"Welcome to Mighty Wash","minutes":4,"why":"Every task on the lot makes more sense once you know what the business is for. Mighty Wash is not selling one wash; it is building a membership business, and members decide every month whether to keep paying. Your greeting, your prep, and your exit check are part of that decision.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Mighty Wash is a local, community-rooted express car wash. Most of our sites are express exterior with free vacuums, and some add Express Interiors. We also run Mighty Express Auto Center for oil, tires, and more.</p>\n<h2>Who we serve</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Members</strong> pay a monthly Membership for unlimited washes. Membership is the engine of the company and the target is 75 to 80% of revenue.</li><li><strong>Retail Customers</strong> pay per wash. Every retail customer is a future member.</li><li><strong>Fleet accounts</strong> are businesses that wash their vehicles with us on an account.</li></ul>\n<h2>Words we use</h2>\n<ul><li>Our three wash packages are <strong>Wonder</strong>, <strong>Super Shine</strong>, and <strong>Mighty Protector</strong>. Know them in order; customers will ask.</li><li>The monthly plan is called <strong>Membership</strong>. Not \"the subscription\" or \"the plan\". A Membership is available for every package.</li><li><strong>MVP</strong> is a Membership plus Express Interiors: unlimited exterior and the inside done too.</li><li><strong>MSA</strong> is a Mighty Sales Associate, the person who signs up Members. On sites that have one, the MSA works from the <strong>Sales Booth</strong>.</li><li><strong>Mighty Max</strong> is our mascot. He is a brush. He shows up at community events and in kids' materials.</li></ul>\n<h2>The Road Map to Success</h2>\n<p>Every role at Mighty Wash is a step on one ladder: Quality Control / Passenger Side Prep, Driver's Side Prep, Tunnel Captain, Supervisor and MSA, Assistant General Manager, General Manager, Regional Manager. Each level has its own standards and its own training in this Academy. Ask your leader where you stand and what it takes to reach the next level.</p>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"eyebrow red\">The one rule</div><p><em>Every customer should feel like the #1 customer of the day.</em></p></div>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"What is the monthly unlimited plan officially called?","o":["The subscription","Membership","The club","The unlimited pass"],"a":1,"why":"Use the word Membership. Customers on it are Members; everyone else is a Retail Customer."},{"q":"What is MVP?","o":["The employee of the month","Unlimited exterior plus Express Interiors","A fleet account","The top retail wash"],"a":1,"why":"MVP is the top Membership: unlimited exterior washes plus Express Interiors."},{"q":"What drives the company forward, according to the standards?","o":["Retail volume","The membership business","Fleet accounts","The auto center"],"a":1,"why":"Membership is the engine. The company target is 75 to 80% of revenue from Members."},{"q":"What are the three wash packages?","o":["Basic, Deluxe, Ultimate","Wonder, Super Shine, Mighty Protector","Bronze, Silver, Gold","Express, Interior, MVP"],"a":1,"why":"Wonder, Super Shine, and Mighty Protector. Each one is available as a Membership."},{"q":"Where does the MSA work on a site that has one?","o":["The tunnel exit","The Sales Booth","The vacuums","Wherever they like"],"a":1,"why":"The MSA works from the Sales Booth where the site is equipped. That is where customers go for Membership."}]},{"key":"p2","title":"Safety on the Lot","minutes":5,"why":"A tunnel moves a vehicle every 20 to 30 seconds and the lot has cars rolling all day. Almost every serious car wash injury comes from one of four things: a moving vehicle, moving equipment, chemicals, or a wet surface. Each rule below exists because someone, somewhere, got hurt without it.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Nothing on this lot is worth getting hurt for. These rules are not optional and they apply to everyone, including managers.</p>\n<h2>Moving vehicles</h2>\n<ul><li>Stand where drivers can see you. Never assume a driver has seen you.</li><li>Never step between the conveyor and a vehicle that is loading or moving.</li><li>Keep your feet clear of the conveyor rollers at all times.</li></ul>\n<h2>Tunnel equipment</h2>\n<ul><li>Never enter the tunnel while equipment is running, for any reason. A dropped towel is not a reason.</li><li>Know where every emergency stop is. Use it the moment you see a person, an animal, or a vehicle problem in the tunnel.</li><li>After an emergency stop, only the Tunnel Captain or shift leader restarts the tunnel, after checking why it stopped.</li></ul>\n<h2>Chemicals</h2>\n<ul><li>Gloves and eye protection whenever you handle concentrate.</li><li>Never mix chemicals. Never pour one into another's container.</li><li>Skin or eye contact: rinse with water right away and tell your leader. Know where the Safety Data Sheets are kept.</li></ul>\n<h2>Surfaces and weather</h2>\n<ul><li>Wet concrete and tunnel floors are slick. Walk, do not run. Wear the approved footwear.</li><li>Lightning in the area: the leader clears the lot. Do not stay outside working.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"eyebrow red\">Report every injury</div><p>Every injury, however small, is reported to your leader on the same shift. A scratch that is not reported cannot be treated or learned from.</p></div>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"A towel falls inside the tunnel while cars are running through. What do you do?","o":["Step in quickly and grab it","Wait for a gap between cars and grab it","Tell the Tunnel Captain; nobody enters until the equipment is stopped","Ignore it until closing"],"a":2,"why":"Nobody enters the tunnel while equipment is running, for any reason. The Tunnel Captain stops it first."},{"q":"Who restarts the tunnel after an emergency stop?","o":["Whoever pressed the stop","The Tunnel Captain or shift leader, after checking the cause","Any team member once the tunnel is clear","The customer in the car"],"a":1,"why":"Only the Tunnel Captain or shift leader restarts, after finding out why it stopped."},{"q":"When do you report an injury?","o":["Only if it needs a doctor","At the end of the week","On the same shift, every time, however small","Only if a customer saw it"],"a":2,"why":"Every injury is reported on the same shift. Small injuries are how we learn about hazards before they cause big ones."},{"q":"You find two chemical jugs with the same label and one is almost empty. What do you do?","o":["Pour the rest into the fuller jug","Leave both and tell your leader","Mix them into a bucket","Rinse one out and refill it from the other"],"a":1,"why":"Never mix or transfer chemicals. Leave them and tell your leader."}]},{"key":"p3","title":"Passenger Side Prep","minutes":5,"why":"The tunnel's chemistry and friction handle most of the dirt. Prep removes what the tunnel reaches least: bugs baked on the front, road film on the lower panels, brake dust in the wheels. Skip the prep and the car leaves with the exact dirt the customer noticed when they decided to come in. A rewash costs a minute of tunnel time and, more important, the customer's confidence.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Your job is to get the dirt the tunnel cannot. You have about the time it takes the car ahead to load, so the order matters.</p>\n<h2>Prep order, passenger side</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li><strong>Front bumper and grille.</strong> Bugs, tar, and the license plate area. This is the first thing the customer looks at.</li><li><strong>Lower panels and rocker.</strong> Road film under the doors, behind the front wheel, and ahead of the rear wheel.</li><li><strong>Wheels and wheel wells.</strong> Brake dust on the face of the wheel and mud in the well.</li><li><strong>Mirror and door handles.</strong> Quick pass.</li><li><strong>Rear bumper and tailgate</strong> if there is time, coordinated with the driver's side.</li></ol>\n<h2>Prep gun technique</h2>\n<ul><li>Keep the tip about a foot from the paint and keep it moving. Holding it on one spot can lift paint or trim.</li><li>Angle the spray across the surface, not straight into seams, badges, or window trim.</li><li>Back off on loose trim, peeling paint, aftermarket wraps, and anything already damaged.</li><li>Hoses stay out of the drive path. A hose across the lane is a trip hazard and a slip hazard.</li></ul>\n<h2>Pace</h2>\n<p>Match the conveyor. On a heavy-soil day, if you cannot finish a car, signal the driver's side and the Tunnel Captain rather than sending it in half-prepped. The tunnel does not get a second chance.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"What is the first area you prep on the passenger side?","o":["Wheels","Front bumper and grille","Rear bumper","Mirror"],"a":1,"why":"The front takes the bugs and is the first thing the customer looks at."},{"q":"Why do lower panels get prepped by hand?","o":["They are the easiest to reach","They are the part of the car tunnel equipment reaches least","The customer cannot see them","They do not need chemical"],"a":1,"why":"Road film sits low where tunnel friction and spray reach least. Prep makes up the difference."},{"q":"You see a piece of trim that is already loose. What do you do with the prep gun?","o":["Blast it to clean under it","Back off and let the tunnel handle it gently; note it to the Tunnel Captain","Hold the spray on it until it is clean","Pull it off so it does not snag"],"a":1,"why":"Pressure on loose trim can finish the damage. Back off, and point it out before the car loads."},{"q":"It is a heavy mud day and you cannot finish prepping a truck in time. What do you do?","o":["Send it in and hope the tunnel gets it","Signal the driver's side and Tunnel Captain so the car is not loaded half-prepped","Skip the wheels","Tell the customer to come back another day"],"a":1,"why":"The tunnel does not get a second chance. Signal rather than send a car in unprepped."}]},{"key":"p4","title":"Quality Control at the Exit","minutes":4,"why":"You are the only person who sees the finished product on every car. The Tunnel Captain sees the equipment, the MSA sees the pay station, and the GM sees the numbers. If a dirty car gets past you, nobody at Mighty Wash sees it again until the customer decides not to come back. The rewash rule exists because a free re-run costs a minute and a lost member costs months.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">The last ten seconds of the wash are yours. Every car gets a look, every time.</p>\n<h2>Inspect every car</h2>\n<ul><li>Rear bumper and tailgate: the most common miss.</li><li>Lower panels and rockers: road film the tunnel did not lift.</li><li>Mirrors and windshield: water spots and dryer misses.</li><li>Wheels: brake dust still on the face.</li></ul>\n<h2>The rewash rule</h2>\n<p>If a car is not clean, <strong>offer a re-run before the customer asks</strong>. \"That rear bumper did not come clean. Pull around and we will run it again, no charge.\" No debate, no judgment about the car. The customer who asks for a rewash is already annoyed; the one you offer it to is impressed.</p>\n<h2>Damage</h2>\n<p>If anything looks unusual at the exit, a hanging piece of trim, a mirror folded wrong, a new mark, tell the Tunnel Captain immediately and note the vehicle. Do not argue with a customer about damage and do not say whose fault it was. Get a leader.</p>\n<h2>Send them off well</h2>\n<p>Wave, make eye contact, and thank them. Point members and first-timers to the vacuums and the interior lane. Keep the exit clear so cars do not bunch up at the dryer.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"A car exits with a dirty rear bumper. The customer has not noticed. What do you do?","o":["Let it go; they did not notice","Offer a re-run before they ask, no charge","Wipe it with a towel while they drive off","Tell the MSA"],"a":1,"why":"Offer the re-run before the customer asks. It is cheaper than a lost member and it builds trust."},{"q":"A customer at the exit says the wash scratched their door. What is your first move?","o":["Explain that the tunnel cannot scratch cars","Apologize for the experience, do not argue or assign fault, note the vehicle, and get the Tunnel Captain","Offer a free wash to end the conversation","Tell them to call corporate"],"a":1,"why":"Never argue and never admit or deny fault. Get a leader and document the vehicle."},{"q":"Which area is the most common miss at the exit?","o":["Hood","Rear bumper and tailgate","Roof","Windshield"],"a":1,"why":"The rear takes the least mechanical action in most tunnels. Check it on every car."},{"q":"Why does the exit need to stay clear?","o":["So the cameras can see the lot","So cars do not bunch up at the dryer and the conveyor keeps moving","So the MSA can sell at the exit","So you can take a break"],"a":1,"why":"Bunching at the exit backs up the dryer and the conveyor. Keep cars moving off the belt."}]},{"key":"p5","title":"Appearance, Conduct, and the Greeting","minutes":4,"why":"A customer cannot see the chemistry or the conveyor. They see you. A phone in your hand says nobody is paying attention; a greeting from across the lot says this place is run well. And the Leadership Manual is clear: the next new hire learns the real standard from what you do, not from what the handbook says.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Your appearance and conduct are part of the product. These are the same rules the GM is held to.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Uniform and dress code</strong> every shift, as issued. Clean, complete, and name visible if your site uses name tags.</li><li><strong>No personal cell phone</strong> on the lot or in front of customers. Phones stay in your locker or vehicle unless a leader says otherwise.</li><li><strong>No vaping</strong> on the lot or in view of customers.</li><li><strong>Greet every customer within the first 10 feet.</strong> Eye contact, a wave, a word. Members by name when you know it.</li><li><strong>Respect</strong> in every interaction, with customers and with each other. No side conversations while a customer is in front of you.</li></ul>\n<h2>When a customer has a problem</h2>\n<p>Listen all the way through. Apologize for the experience. If you can fix it on the spot, do it. If you cannot, get a leader and stay with the customer until the leader arrives. Never send a customer away to \"call someone\".</p>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"eyebrow red\">You set a standard too</div><p><em>Whatever you let slide in front of a new hire becomes their version of normal. The standard you walk past is the standard you accept.</em></p></div>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"How soon should a customer be greeted?","o":["When they reach the pay station","Within the first 10 feet of the lot","After the wash","Only if they look at you"],"a":1,"why":"Greet every customer within the first 10 feet, every shift."},{"q":"Where is your personal phone during a shift?","o":["In your pocket on silent","Off the lot, unless a leader says otherwise","In your hand if the lot is empty","Clipped to your belt"],"a":1,"why":"No personal phone on the lot or in front of customers, even when the lot is empty. The cameras and the team both notice."},{"q":"A customer has a complaint you cannot fix. What do you do?","o":["Tell them to call corporate","Listen, apologize, get a leader, and stay with them until the leader arrives","Offer a free wash and move on","Explain company policy"],"a":1,"why":"Listen, apologize, get a leader, and stay with the customer. Never send them away to call someone."},{"q":"A veteran coworker vapes behind the tunnel out of sight. Why does it still matter?","o":["It does not, if customers cannot see","The standard is no vaping on the lot, and the next new hire learns normal from what they see","Only the GM has to follow conduct rules","It only matters on camera"],"a":1,"why":"The rule is the lot, not the line of sight. And what new hires see tolerated becomes their standard."}]},{"key":"p6","title":"Opening, Closing, and Site Readiness","minutes":4,"why":"The Regional Manager checks three things on camera every morning: did the site open on time, did a test car run before the first customer, and was the site cleaned the night before. Those three are checked daily because they are the foundation of everything else. A site that opens late and dirty has already lost the day's first members.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">A customer-ready site is built the night before and confirmed the morning of.</p>\n<h2>Closing: the nightly clean</h2>\n<ul><li>Trash cans emptied and relined, including the vacuum area.</li><li>Vacuum area blown or swept, hoses hung, nozzles checked.</li><li>Pay stations and menu boards wiped. Screens clean.</li><li>Tunnel bay and exit hosed down. Towels restocked.</li><li>Restrooms cleaned and stocked if your site has them.</li><li>Anything broken or missing written down for the leader.</li></ul>\n<h2>Opening</h2>\n<ul><li>The site opens on time, every day. If it will be late for any reason, the leader reports it to leadership immediately.</li><li>The Tunnel Captain runs a <strong>test car</strong> before the first customer. You check it at the exit like any other car.</li><li>Walk the lot once: trash, hoses, signs, anything a customer would notice.</li></ul>\n<h2>During the day</h2>\n<p>Report maintenance issues that affect customers (a dead vacuum, a flickering sign, a leaking hose) to your leader right away. Follow up if it is still broken tomorrow.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"When does the test car run?","o":["After the first customer","Before the first customer","At noon","Only on Mondays"],"a":1,"why":"A test car runs before the first customer so the first paying car gets a proven wash."},{"q":"Who verifies opening time, the test car, and the nightly clean?","o":["The GM, by walking the lot","The Regional Manager, by camera each morning","Corporate, monthly","Nobody; it is on the honor system"],"a":1,"why":"The RM checks all three by camera every morning."},{"q":"When is the thorough site clean done?","o":["First thing in the morning","The night before, so the site opens customer-ready","During slow periods","Once a week"],"a":1,"why":"The nightly clean is done the night before so the site opens customer-ready."},{"q":"A vacuum is dead and customers are noticing. What do you do?","o":["Put an out-of-order sign on it and move on","Report it to your leader promptly and follow up if it is still down tomorrow","Try to fix it yourself","Wait for the RM visit"],"a":1,"why":"Maintenance affecting customer experience is reported promptly and followed until resolved."}]}]}],"scenarios":[{"t":"The dropped towel","s":"A drying towel blows off the rack into the tunnel. Cars are running through. The Tunnel Captain is at the far end of the lot.","o":["Wait for a gap between cars and dart in to grab it.","Call or signal the Tunnel Captain and wait. Nobody enters until the equipment is stopped.","Grab it with a broom handle from the entrance.","Leave it until closing."],"a":1,"why":"Nobody enters a running tunnel for any reason. Signal the Tunnel Captain; it comes out when the equipment stops."},{"t":"Bugs on the bumper","s":"A car comes off the belt with bug residue still on the front bumper. The customer is already pulling toward the vacuums and has not noticed.","o":["Let it go. They did not notice.","Wave them down and offer a re-run before they ask, no charge.","Mention it to the MSA so she can offer a discount next time.","Wipe it with a towel as they roll by."],"a":1,"why":"Offer the re-run before the customer asks. A free minute of tunnel time beats a member who quietly decides the wash does not work."},{"t":"The scratch","s":"At the exit, a customer gets out and points at a mark on his door. He is angry and says the wash did it.","o":["Tell him the tunnel cannot scratch paint.","Apologize for the experience without saying whose fault it is, ask him to pull aside, note the vehicle, and get the Tunnel Captain.","Offer a free wash so he leaves.","Hand him the corporate phone number."],"a":1,"why":"Never argue and never admit or deny fault. Document the vehicle and get a leader. The claim process starts from there."},{"t":"Empty lot, buzzing phone","s":"It is a slow Tuesday afternoon. No cars on the lot. Your phone buzzes in your pocket.","o":["Check it quickly; there are no customers.","Leave it. No personal phone on the lot, and the camera and the new hire across the lot both see what normal looks like.","Step behind the tunnel to check it.","Check it only if it buzzes twice."],"a":1,"why":"The rule is the lot, not the customer count. A new hire who sees you check it learns that the phone rule is optional."},{"t":"Skip the restrooms","s":"Closing time. A tenured coworker says the restrooms looked fine this morning and you can skip them tonight.","o":["Skip them; he has been here longer.","Clean them to standard. The nightly clean is the whole list, and the RM sees the result on camera and on the next visit.","Clean them halfway.","Report him to the GM."],"a":1,"why":"The nightly clean is the full checklist. Tenure does not change the standard, and the site opens customer-ready or it does not."}],"assessment":[{"q":"Membership should be what share of company revenue?","o":["25 to 35%","50%","75 to 80%","100%"],"a":2},{"q":"Who restarts the tunnel after an emergency stop?","o":["Anyone once it is clear","The Tunnel Captain or shift leader, after checking the cause","The customer","The MSA"],"a":1},{"q":"When is an injury reported?","o":["If it needs treatment","Same shift, every time","Weekly","Never for minor ones"],"a":1},{"q":"First area to prep on the passenger side?","o":["Wheels","Front bumper and grille","Rear","Roof"],"a":1},{"q":"A car exits dirty. The customer has not noticed. You ...","o":["let it go","offer a re-run before they ask","wipe it with a towel","tell the MSA"],"a":1},{"q":"A customer claims damage at the exit. You ...","o":["explain it was not the wash","apologize for the experience, do not assign fault, note the vehicle, get a leader","offer a free wash","give them the corporate number"],"a":1},{"q":"Greet every customer within ...","o":["the first 10 feet","30 seconds","the pay station","the exit"],"a":0},{"q":"Personal phones on the lot are ...","o":["fine when it is slow","not allowed","allowed on silent","allowed for managers"],"a":1},{"q":"The test car runs ...","o":["after the first customer","before the first customer","at closing","weekly"],"a":1},{"q":"The thorough site clean happens ...","o":["in the morning","the night before","when it is slow","on Mondays"],"a":1}]},{"key":"l2","num":2,"title":"Driver's Side Prep","short":"Driver's Side Prep","role":"You load every vehicle onto the conveyor, prep the driver's side, and are the last safety check before the tunnel.","blurb":"Loading, driver-side prep, damage pre-check, pace, member handoffs, and teaching the passenger side.","why":"Loading is where most tunnel incidents and most damage claims begin. The driver's side prep is also the first face-to-face moment with the customer. This level is about doing both well at pace.","draft":true,"pass":80,"ack_formal":false,"ack_title":null,"ack_statement":"I have completed the Driver's Side Prep training. I can load vehicles safely with full instructions and signals, prep to standard at pace, pre-check for damage, hand off first-timers, and teach the passenger side standard to a new hire.","courses":[{"title":"Driver's Side Prep","blurb":null,"note":null,"draft":false,"lessons":[{"key":"d1","title":"Loading the Tunnel","minutes":6,"why":"A vehicle loaded wrong can jump the roller, hit the car ahead, or drag equipment. Almost every tunnel collision traces back to a loading mistake: a car in gear, a wheel off the guide, a mirror left out, a gap that closed. The loader is the last safety check before the belt, and the instructions you give the driver are the only ones they will get.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">You guide every vehicle onto the conveyor. The customer is trusting you to tell them exactly what to do.</p>\n<h2>The driver instructions, every car</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li><strong>\"Pull forward slowly, follow my hand.\"</strong> Guide the driver-side tire onto the conveyor guide rail.</li><li><strong>\"Stop.\"</strong> Clear stop signal, palm up.</li><li><strong>\"Put it in neutral, hands off the wheel, foot off the brake.\"</strong> Say all three, every time, even to members.</li><li><strong>\"Mirrors in, wipers off, windows up.\"</strong> Fold the driver mirror yourself if the customer cannot.</li></ol>\n<h2>Hand signals</h2>\n<p>Use the same signals every time: come forward (wave in), stop (palm out), turn left or right (point). Big, slow, visible. Stand where the driver can see you in the windshield, never beside the front wheel.</p>\n<h2>Spacing</h2>\n<p>Load on the roller the Tunnel Captain calls. Too close and vehicles touch in the tunnel; too far and throughput drops. At peak, one roller gap is the standard unless the Captain says otherwise.</p>\n<h2>What does not go in</h2>\n<ul><li>Vehicles over the posted height or width, or that exceed the site's lift and tire limits.</li><li>Trailers, hitches that will catch equipment, bike racks, and open beds with loose cargo.</li><li>Roof cargo, ladders, loose trim, cracked windshields, and anything the customer will not let you fold or remove.</li><li>When in doubt, call the Tunnel Captain. A turned-away car is a conversation; a damaged car is a claim.</li></ul>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"Which three instructions does every driver get before loading?","o":["Windows down, radio off, lights on","Neutral, hands off the wheel, foot off the brake","Park, parking brake on, engine off","Drive, foot on brake, steer straight"],"a":1,"why":"Neutral, hands off the wheel, foot off the brake. Say all three to every driver, including members who have heard it a hundred times."},{"q":"Where do you stand while guiding a vehicle onto the guide rail?","o":["Beside the front wheel","Where the driver can see you through the windshield","Behind the vehicle","Inside the tunnel entrance"],"a":1,"why":"Stand in the driver's line of sight and clear of the wheels and the conveyor."},{"q":"A pickup arrives with a loaded open bed and a ball hitch. What do you do?","o":["Load it; the tunnel can handle it","Do not load it until the Tunnel Captain checks it; loose cargo and hitches damage equipment and vehicles","Load it with extra spacing","Ask the customer to hold the cargo down"],"a":1,"why":"Open beds with loose cargo and hitches are on the do-not-load list. Call the Captain."},{"q":"What is the standard spacing at peak?","o":["Bumper to bumper","One roller gap unless the Tunnel Captain says otherwise","Three rollers","Whatever the driver prefers"],"a":1,"why":"One roller gap keeps throughput up without vehicles touching in the tunnel."}]},{"key":"d2","title":"Driver's Side Prep Standard","minutes":4,"why":"The driver's side is the side the customer gets out on. It is also the side they see first at the exit. The driver-side prep carries the same areas as the passenger side plus the greeting and the loading, so the sequence has to be tight or the belt starves.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Same prep standard as the passenger side, plus the greeting and the load. The order makes it fit.</p>\n<h2>Sequence per car</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li><strong>Greet</strong> as the car approaches. Eye contact, \"Welcome to Mighty Wash.\"</li><li><strong>Prep</strong> front bumper and grille, driver-side lower panels, wheels and wells, mirror and handles. Rear bumper if the passenger side has not reached it.</li><li><strong>Load</strong> with the full driver instructions and hand signals.</li><li><strong>Confirm</strong> neutral and hands off before you wave the next car up.</li></ol>\n<h2>Coordination</h2>\n<ul><li>Call the rear: whichever side reaches the rear bumper first says so. Do not both skip it.</li><li>On a heavy-soil day, agree with the passenger side and the Tunnel Captain whether to slow the belt rather than send cars in unprepped.</li><li>Wipers: confirm off before the car enters. A wiper arm caught in the mitter is a claim.</li></ul>\n<h2>Technique reminders</h2>\n<p>A foot from the paint, keep moving, angle across seams, back off on loose trim and damaged paint. Hoses out of the lane.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"What is the sequence per car on the driver's side?","o":["Load, prep, greet","Greet, prep, load, confirm","Prep, greet, load","Load, confirm, prep"],"a":1,"why":"Greet as they approach, prep, load with full instructions, confirm neutral and hands off, then wave up the next car."},{"q":"Both sides are busy and neither has reached the rear bumper. What is the rule?","o":["Skip it; the tunnel gets the rear","Call it: whichever side reaches it first says so, so it is never skipped by both","The passenger side always does the rear","The driver side always does the rear"],"a":1,"why":"Call the rear out loud so it is never skipped by both sides."},{"q":"Why confirm the wipers are off before the car enters?","o":["It saves the customer's battery","A wiper arm caught in the tunnel equipment is a damage claim","The sensors need it","It is not important"],"a":1,"why":"Wiper arms snag cloth and mitters. Confirm off, every car."}]},{"key":"d3","title":"Reading the Car: Damage Pre-Check","minutes":4,"why":"Most damage claims are about damage that was already there. A thirty-second look before loading, and one sentence to the customer, turns a dispute into a conversation that already happened. It also keeps real problems, a loose bumper cover or a cracked mirror, out of the tunnel where they become real damage.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Look at every car before it loads, and say what you see.</p>\n<h2>What to look for</h2>\n<ul><li>Loose or hanging trim, bumper covers, side moldings, and emblems.</li><li>Cracked or loose mirrors, mirror caps, and antennas.</li><li>Aftermarket parts: spoilers, light bars, bug deflectors, wraps, and tint peeling at the edges.</li><li>Pre-existing scratches, dents, and rust where paint could lift.</li><li>Roof racks, cargo boxes, and anything strapped on.</li></ul>\n<h2>When in doubt, point it out</h2>\n<p>Say it to the customer before loading: \"I noticed your mirror cap is loose. The wash could pull it. Do you want to go ahead, or take it off first?\" Then tell the Tunnel Captain. If the customer chooses to proceed, the Captain decides whether to load. A customer who heard you say it before the wash does not argue about it after.</p>\n<h2>Flag it, do not just remember it</h2>\n<p>Anything you flag gets to the Tunnel Captain before the car loads. If your site uses a pre-wash note or a tag, use it. Memory does not hold up in a claim; a note does.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"You see a loose mirror cap before loading. What do you do?","o":["Load the car; it is probably fine","Tell the customer what you see and why it matters, then tell the Tunnel Captain before loading","Pull the cap off and hand it to the customer","Load with extra spacing"],"a":1,"why":"Say it to the customer before the wash, then flag it to the Captain. The Captain decides whether to load."},{"q":"Why does the pre-check matter for claims?","o":["It proves the tunnel is safe","Most claims are about damage that was already there; a pre-check and one sentence turn a dispute into a conversation that already happened","It is required by insurance","It lets you refuse cars"],"a":1,"why":"A customer who heard you point it out before the wash does not argue about it after."},{"q":"Where does a flagged item go?","o":["In your memory","To the Tunnel Captain before the car loads, and on the site's pre-wash note or tag","To the MSA","To corporate"],"a":1,"why":"Flag it to the Captain before loading and record it. Memory does not hold up in a claim."}]},{"key":"d4","title":"Pace and Throughput","minutes":4,"why":"On a Saturday the line is the business. Every empty roller is a car that waited longer, and a long line is the number one reason customers pull out and leave. Pace is not rushing; it is a rhythm where prep, load, and belt speed match so the tunnel never starves and never jams.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">The tunnel can only wash what you load. Your rhythm sets the site's capacity.</p>\n<h2>The rhythm</h2>\n<ul><li>The Tunnel Captain sets belt speed for the conditions. Your prep time per car has to fit it. On a clean-car day that is quick; on a mud day the Captain slows the belt rather than you skipping prep.</li><li>Keep one car staged behind the one you are prepping. Greet it while the prep gun is still in your hand.</li><li>Never let the belt run empty while cars are waiting. If you are falling behind, say so; the Captain or a Supervisor can add a prepper.</li></ul>\n<h2>Watch the line</h2>\n<p>When the line backs up past the pay station, tell the MSA so they can work the line and keep customers from pulling out. When the vacuums are full, tell the exit so they can direct traffic.</p>\n<h2>Pace never beats safety</h2>\n<p>A fast load with a car in gear is slower than a careful one, because the stop that follows costs five minutes. Instructions and signals do not get shortened at peak.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"Who sets belt speed?","o":["The loader","The Tunnel Captain, for the conditions","The customer","The GM, by schedule"],"a":1,"why":"The Captain sets belt speed. Prep fits the belt; on a heavy-soil day the belt slows rather than prep being skipped."},{"q":"You are falling behind at peak. What do you do?","o":["Skip the wheels to catch up","Say so; the Captain or Supervisor can add a prepper","Shorten the driver instructions","Load two cars at once"],"a":1,"why":"Speak up. Adding a prepper is the answer; shortcuts on prep or instructions are not."},{"q":"The line is backing up past the pay station. Who needs to know?","o":["Nobody; it will clear","The MSA, so they can work the line and keep customers from leaving","The customers","Corporate"],"a":1,"why":"The MSA can work the line, answer questions, and keep customers from pulling out."}]},{"key":"d5","title":"Members and First-Timers on the Lot","minutes":4,"why":"Conversion happens at the pay station, but the handoff happens on the lot. You can see who is new before the MSA can. A first-timer who is greeted, helped, and introduced to the MSA is far more likely to join than one who figures out the menu alone. And a member who is thanked by name stays longer.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">You are not the salesperson, but you are the first step of the sale.</p>\n<h2>Members</h2>\n<ul><li>The pay station recognizes members by tag or plate. If you can see the name or the member lane, use it: \"Thanks for being a member, Mr. Garza.\"</li><li>Members are the base. Treat every one like the #1 customer of the day.</li></ul>\n<h2>Spotting first-timers</h2>\n<ul><li>Reading the menu board slowly, hesitating at the pay station, asking what the packages are, or asking \"how does this work?\"</li><li>Out-of-town plates and rental cars.</li></ul>\n<h2>The handoff</h2>\n<p>Point them to the Sales Booth, or wave the MSA over on a site without one: \"First time? Sarah in the booth can get you set up. Members wash unlimited for about the price of two washes.\" Then let the MSA do the W.A.S.H. You do not need to pitch, and you never pressure. Your job is to make sure every first-timer meets an MSA.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"What is your role in the sale as a prepper?","o":["Close the membership at the loading point","Spot first-timers and hand them to an MSA; never pressure","Explain all three packages","None; stay out of sales"],"a":1,"why":"Spot and hand off. The MSA runs W.A.S.H.; you make sure every first-timer meets one."},{"q":"Which of these is a sign of a first-timer?","o":["Member lane","Reading the menu board slowly and hesitating at the pay station","Folded mirrors","A clean car"],"a":1,"why":"Hesitation at the menu or pay station, questions about how it works, out-of-town plates."},{"q":"How do you treat a member you recognize?","o":["Skip the greeting; they know the drill","Thank them by name when you can; they are the base","Tell them about MVP","Move them to the front"],"a":1,"why":"Members are the base. Thank them by name when you can and give them the full standard."}]},{"key":"d6","title":"Teaching the Passenger Side","minutes":4,"why":"Most new hires at Mighty Wash learn the job from the driver's side prepper, not from a manager. The Leadership Manual says a new hire builds their model of normal from whoever they stand next to in the first shifts. That is you. How you teach the passenger side decides what standard the site has in two weeks.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">When a new QC / Passenger Side Prep starts, you are their first model of what normal looks like.</p>\n<h2>Set the standard in specific words</h2>\n<p>Not \"keep the car clean.\" Instead: \"Front bumper first, then lower panels, then wheels. Every car.\" Specific standards can be checked; vague ones cannot.</p>\n<h2>Show, then watch</h2>\n<ul><li>Do the first few cars yourself while they watch. Narrate what you are doing and why.</li><li>Then they do it while you watch. Correct in the moment, out loud, with the standard: \"Lower panels first on this one, the film is heavy.\"</li><li>Recognize it when they get it right. \"That bumper was perfect.\" People repeat what gets noticed.</li></ul>\n<h2>Hold it from day one</h2>\n<p>A new hire will hold your standard until they see one exception go by. If you skip the wheel wells when it is busy, that is the standard they learned. Enforce it evenly for yourself first.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"Which is a specific, teachable standard?","o":["\"Do a good job on every car\"","\"Front bumper first, then lower panels, then wheels, every car\"","\"Be customer-focused\"","\"Use your judgment\""],"a":1,"why":"Specific standards can be observed and corrected. Vague ones cannot."},{"q":"How do you correct a new hire who skipped the wheels?","o":["Mention it at the end of the week","In the moment, out loud, with the standard","Report it to the Supervisor","Do the wheels yourself without saying anything"],"a":1,"why":"Correct in the moment and say the standard, so the correction reads as the rule."},{"q":"A new hire watches you skip the wheel wells on a busy day. What did they learn?","o":["That you are efficient","That wheel wells are optional when it is busy","Nothing; they were not paying attention","That the Captain sets the pace"],"a":1,"why":"They learned the real standard from what they saw. Enforce it on yourself first."}]}]}],"scenarios":[{"t":"Lifted truck with a roof box","s":"A lifted pickup with oversized tires and a cargo box on the roof pulls up. The customer says he washes here all the time.","o":["Load it; he says he has done it before.","Check it against the site limits and call the Tunnel Captain before loading. If it is outside limits, it does not go in, politely.","Load it with extra spacing.","Ask him to drive through without the belt."],"a":1,"why":"Site limits do not change because a customer says so. The Captain decides, and a turned-away car is a conversation while a damaged one is a claim."},{"t":"Foot on the brake","s":"A customer keeps her foot on the brake after you have given the instructions. The car is on the roller and the belt is moving.","o":["Wave the next car up; she will figure it out.","Stop signal, repeat the instruction clearly and make sure she takes her foot off before the roller engages. If she will not, signal the Captain to stop.","Knock on the window and move on.","Let it go; the roller will push through."],"a":1,"why":"A car braking against the roller is how vehicles jump the belt. Repeat the instruction, confirm, and stop the belt if needed."},{"t":"The growing gap","s":"It is Saturday. The passenger side prepper is chatting with a customer and the belt has run empty for two rollers while six cars wait.","o":["Say nothing; it is not your job to correct them.","Say it in the moment: \"Belt is empty, let's go,\" and keep loading. Mention it to the Supervisor if it keeps happening.","Report them to the GM after the shift.","Slow down to match them."],"a":1,"why":"Correct in the moment, out loud, with the standard. An empty belt at peak is lost cars."},{"t":"Reading the menu","s":"A car with out-of-state plates sits at the pay station for a long time. The driver is scrolling the screen and looking at the menu board.","o":["Wait; they will pick something.","Walk over, greet them, and bring the MSA in: \"First time? Sarah can get you set up.\"","Pick Mighty Protector for them so the line moves.","Tell them to pull forward so the line moves."],"a":1,"why":"That is a first-timer. Your job is the handoff to the MSA, not the pitch."},{"t":"The new hire and the wheel wells","s":"The new QC prepper you are training skips the wheel wells on three cars in a row. It is busy and you are loading.","o":["Let it go until it slows down.","Between cars, say the standard out loud: \"Wheels and wells every car, even when it is busy,\" and watch the next one.","Do the wells yourself from your side.","Tell the Supervisor at the end of the day."],"a":1,"why":"Correct in the moment with the standard. They are building their model of normal right now."}],"assessment":[{"q":"Every driver is told: neutral, hands off the wheel, and ...","o":["lights on","foot off the brake","radio off","engine off"],"a":1},{"q":"Standard spacing at peak is ...","o":["bumper to bumper","one roller gap unless the Captain says otherwise","three rollers","the customer's choice"],"a":1},{"q":"An open bed with loose cargo ...","o":["loads normally","does not load until the Captain checks it","loads with extra spacing","loads if the customer insists"],"a":1},{"q":"Before the car enters, confirm the wipers are ...","o":["on","off","on intermittent","does not matter"],"a":1},{"q":"You see a loose mirror cap before loading. First you ...","o":["load it","say it to the customer and flag the Captain before loading","remove it","ignore it"],"a":1},{"q":"Belt speed is set by ...","o":["the loader","the Tunnel Captain","the customer","the schedule"],"a":1},{"q":"Falling behind at peak, you ...","o":["skip prep","say so and get a prepper added","shorten instructions","load two cars"],"a":1},{"q":"Your role in membership sales is to ...","o":["close the sale","spot first-timers and hand them to an MSA","explain pricing","stay out of it"],"a":1},{"q":"A new hire learns the real standard from ...","o":["the handbook","what they see you do in the first shifts","the GM","the quiz"],"a":1},{"q":"A correction should be given ...","o":["at the weekly meeting","in the moment, out loud, with the standard","by the GM only","in writing"],"a":1}]},{"key":"l3","num":3,"title":"Tunnel Captain","short":"Tunnel Captain","role":"You own the wash process from the loading point to the exit: equipment, chemistry, stops, quality, and the lot crew when no Supervisor is on.","blurb":"Startup and shutdown, chemistry and quality, stops and jams, damage claims, and leading the crew.","why":"The Tunnel Captain is the first level where you are responsible for a result you do not produce with your own hands. Clean, dry, safe cars out the exit, all day, is the job. Everything in this level is how that happens.","draft":true,"pass":80,"ack_formal":false,"ack_title":null,"ack_statement":"I have completed the Tunnel Captain training. I can run startup and shutdown, read and correct wash quality, manage a stop safely, document a damage claim, and lead the lot crew to the Mighty Wash standard.","courses":[{"title":"Tunnel Captain","blurb":null,"note":null,"draft":false,"lessons":[{"key":"t1","title":"Owning the Tunnel","minutes":4,"why":"A tunnel that is down costs every car in the line and every member who drives past the closed sign. A tunnel that is up but washing poorly costs more slowly and quietly. The Captain is the person on the lot who can see both coming, which is why this level exists between prep and supervision.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">The Tunnel Captain owns four things: the equipment runs, the chemistry is right, the cars come out clean and dry, and nobody gets hurt in the tunnel.</p>\n<h2>Your day</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Before open:</strong> startup checklist and the test car.</li><li><strong>During the day:</strong> walk the tunnel every hour. Listen for changes. Watch the exit. Set belt speed for the conditions.</li><li><strong>When something goes wrong:</strong> you own the stop, the recovery, and the report.</li><li><strong>Close:</strong> shutdown checklist, chemical levels for tomorrow, and the log.</li></ul>\n<h2>Lead when no Supervisor is on</h2>\n<p>When you are the senior person on the lot, you are the qualified leader on that shift. Assignments, breaks, pace, corrections, and customer escalations come to you until a Supervisor or manager arrives.</p>\n<h2>Log it</h2>\n<p>Every stop, every chemical change, every maintenance issue goes in the daily log and, if it touched a customer, in the Notes feature. The GM and the RM manage from what is written down.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"What are the four things the Tunnel Captain owns?","o":["Sales, labor, scheduling, and admin","Equipment up, chemistry right, clean dry cars, and nobody hurt in the tunnel","Opening, closing, the cash drawer, and the schedule","Prep, loading, the exit, and vacuums"],"a":1,"why":"Equipment runs, chemistry is right, cars come out clean and dry, and nobody gets hurt in the tunnel."},{"q":"How often do you walk the tunnel during the day?","o":["Once at open","Every hour","Only when something sounds wrong","At close"],"a":1,"why":"Every hour. Changes in sound, spray pattern, or foam are the early warning."},{"q":"A Supervisor calls out and you are the senior person on the lot. What are you?","o":["Still just the Captain","The qualified leader on that shift until a Supervisor or manager arrives","Off the hook for the exit","In charge of the pay station only"],"a":1,"why":"Every shift needs a qualified leader. When it is you, assignments, pace, corrections, and escalations are yours."}]},{"key":"t2","title":"Startup and Shutdown","minutes":6,"why":"The RM checks the test car on camera every morning because a test car proves the whole system before a paying customer is in it. The startup checklist is what makes the test car pass. Shutdown is tomorrow's startup: chemical that ran out overnight is a tunnel that opens late.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Startup is a fixed sequence. Do it the same way every day so a miss stands out.</p>\n<h2>Startup checklist</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li><strong>Air and water.</strong> Compressor at operating pressure, no leaks. Water supply and reclaim system on and at pressure.</li><li><strong>Chemicals.</strong> Every drum has enough for the day. Lines primed. No dry tips. If a drum will not make it through peak, swap it now, not at 2 pm.</li><li><strong>Conveyor.</strong> Walk it. Rollers up and down cleanly, no debris on the belt, guide rail clear. Run it empty.</li><li><strong>Friction and mitters.</strong> Cloth and brushes intact, nothing tangled, spinning free.</li><li><strong>Arches and nozzles.</strong> Spray patterns even. A plugged nozzle shows up as a streak on the test car.</li><li><strong>Dryers.</strong> All blowers running, no rattles.</li><li><strong>Lights, signage, pay stations, vacuums.</strong> Everything a customer touches.</li><li><strong>Test car.</strong> Run it before the first customer. Inspect it at the exit like a customer's car. Fix what it shows before opening.</li></ol>\n<h2>Shutdown checklist</h2>\n<ul><li>Belt and equipment stopped and powered down per site procedure.</li><li>Chemical levels noted for tomorrow. Anything low on the order list tonight.</li><li>Tunnel bay hosed, drains clear, reclaim pit checked.</li><li>Any issue from the day written in the log for the GM.</li></ul>\n<h2>Late open</h2>\n<p>If the test car fails or equipment will not start, the leader reports to leadership immediately. A late open is never hidden; the RM sees it on camera anyway.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"A drum of presoak will probably run out mid-afternoon. When do you swap it?","o":["When it runs out","At startup, before peak","At shutdown","Next week"],"a":1,"why":"Swap it at startup. A tunnel that loses chemical at 2 pm on a Saturday washes poorly for an hour before anyone notices."},{"q":"What does the test car prove?","o":["That the pay station works","The whole system, before a paying customer is in it","That the lot is clean","That the crew is on time"],"a":1,"why":"The test car proves the system end to end. Inspect it at the exit like any customer's car."},{"q":"A plugged nozzle usually shows up as ...","o":["a loud noise","a streak on the test car","a late open","nothing visible"],"a":1,"why":"Uneven spray leaves a streak. The test car finds it before a customer does."},{"q":"The tunnel will not start and the site will open late. What happens?","o":["Open quietly when it is fixed","The leader reports to leadership immediately","Wait until the RM calls","Send customers to another site"],"a":1,"why":"Late openings are reported immediately. The RM sees opening time on camera regardless."}]},{"key":"t3","title":"Chemistry and a Clean Car","minutes":5,"why":"A clean car is five things working together: the right chemical, enough dwell time, mechanical friction, a full rinse, and a dry finish. When quality drops, it is almost always one of the five, and the Captain is the person who can tell which. Guessing wastes chemical and washes cars poorly while you guess.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">You do not need to be a chemist. You need to recognize the signs and know what to check.</p>\n<h2>The five parts of a clean car</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>Part</th><th>What it does</th><th>When it is off, you see</th></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Chemical</td><td>Presoak loosens film; soap carries it; drying agent and wax finish it</td><td>Film left on lower panels, poor foam, no beading</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Dwell</td><td>Chemical needs seconds on the surface before friction</td><td>Clean on top, dirty low; belt too fast for the soil</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Friction</td><td>Cloth and brushes lift what chemical loosened</td><td>Streaks in a pattern, same spot every car</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Rinse</td><td>Removes chemical and dirt before drying</td><td>Haze, residue, spots after drying</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Dry</td><td>Drying agent plus blowers</td><td>Water sheets, drips from mirrors, wet rear</td></tr></table></div>\n<h2>Rules</h2>\n<ul><li>Check concentration on the schedule the vendor and GM set for your site. Record it.</li><li>Never dilute a drum to stretch it, and never mix products.</li><li>Low chemical is called in before it is out. Out of chemical on a Saturday is a GM conversation.</li><li>When quality drops mid-day: check the exit, then the chemical tips and flows, then the belt speed, then friction. In that order.</li></ul>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"Cars are coming out clean on top but filmy on the lower panels. Most likely cause?","o":["Dryers","Not enough dwell or presoak reaching the lower panels","The pay station","Too much rinse"],"a":1,"why":"Clean high and dirty low points to chemical and dwell on the lower panels. Check presoak flow and belt speed."},{"q":"A streak shows up in the same place on every car. Most likely cause?","o":["Chemical concentration","A friction or nozzle issue in a fixed position","The customer's paint","Dwell time"],"a":1,"why":"Same spot, every car, is mechanical: a plugged nozzle, a tangled mitter, a brush out of position."},{"q":"A drum is nearly out and the delivery is tomorrow. What do you do?","o":["Dilute it to stretch it","Call it in now and tell the GM; never dilute or mix","Mix in a similar product","Run without it"],"a":1,"why":"Never dilute or mix. Call it in before it is out, and tell the GM so the site is not surprised."},{"q":"Quality drops mid-day. What is the first check?","o":["Belt speed","The exit, to see what the cars actually look like","Friction","Chemical order"],"a":1,"why":"Look at the cars first. The symptom tells you which of the five to check next."}]},{"key":"t4","title":"Stops, Jams, and Recovery","minutes":5,"why":"A stop handled well is a five-minute delay and a story the customer tells about how calm the crew was. A stop handled badly is an injury or a damaged car. The sequence below exists so that nobody has to improvise with a vehicle on the belt and a line out to the street.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Stop first, think second, restart last.</p>\n<h2>When to stop</h2>\n<ul><li>A person or animal in the tunnel. Always.</li><li>A vehicle off the roller, turned on the belt, or touching the vehicle ahead.</li><li>A door, hatch, or hood opening. Brake lights on and holding.</li><li>Equipment noise, smoke, or a piece of equipment hanging into the path.</li></ul>\n<h2>Recovery sequence</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li><strong>Stop the belt.</strong> Emergency stop. Say \"Stopping\" to the lot.</li><li><strong>Customers stay in their cars.</strong> Tell them through the window. Nobody gets out in the tunnel.</li><li><strong>Assess from outside the path.</strong> Never go under or between vehicles on the belt.</li><li><strong>Clear it with two people.</strong> One directs, one acts. Back a vehicle off a roller only with the driver following your signals.</li><li><strong>Check equipment</strong> before restart: nothing tangled, nothing hanging, belt clear.</li><li><strong>Restart</strong> only you or the shift leader, with a \"Restarting\" call to the lot.</li><li><strong>Log it</strong> and tell the GM: what happened, which car, any damage, how long down.</li></ol>\n<h2>Talk to the line</h2>\n<p>Send someone to the pay station and the line: \"Short stop, two minutes, we will have you through.\" Customers wait for information; they leave for silence.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"A customer's hatch opens in the tunnel. What do you do first?","o":["Call to them to close it","Stop the belt","Run in and close it","Speed up to get them through"],"a":1,"why":"Stop first. Then instruct the customer through the window. Nobody enters a running tunnel."},{"q":"During a jam, where do customers stay?","o":["They should get out and wait at the exit","In their cars","In the tunnel walkway","At the pay station"],"a":1,"why":"Customers stay in their vehicles. Nobody gets out in the tunnel."},{"q":"How many people clear a vehicle off a roller?","o":["One, quickly","Two: one directs, one acts, with the driver following signals","Three","The customer alone"],"a":1,"why":"Two people, with the driver following signals. Nobody goes under or between vehicles on the belt."},{"q":"After the restart, what is left to do?","o":["Nothing","Log it and tell the GM: what, which car, any damage, how long down","Apologize to the whole line","Reset the chemical"],"a":1,"why":"Every stop is logged and reported. The GM and RM manage from what is written."}]},{"key":"t5","title":"Damage Claims","minutes":5,"why":"How the first five minutes of a claim go decides whether it costs the company a conversation or a check. A customer who is heard, who sees you take photos and pull the camera, and who gets a clear next step usually stays a customer. A customer who is argued with calls corporate angry, and now it costs twice.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">You are not deciding the claim. You are making sure it can be decided fairly, and keeping the customer.</p>\n<h2>The first five minutes</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li><strong>Move them aside</strong> so the exit keeps flowing, and give them your full attention.</li><li><strong>Listen all the way through.</strong> Then: \"I am sorry this happened on your visit. Let me get this documented properly.\"</li><li><strong>Do not admit or deny fault.</strong> Not \"the wash did that\" and not \"that was already there.\" Both are decisions you do not make.</li><li><strong>Photograph</strong> the damage, the whole panel, and the vehicle from four corners. Note plate, time, and which wash package.</li><li><strong>Pull the camera footage</strong> of the load and the tunnel while it is fresh, or tell the GM to.</li><li><strong>Fill out the incident form</strong> and put the interaction in Notes.</li><li><strong>Give the next step:</strong> \"Our GM reviews every claim and will contact you by [when].\" Get their name and number.</li><li><strong>Escalate same day</strong> to the Supervisor or GM. Corporate makes the decision.</li></ol>\n<h2>What not to say</h2>\n<p>\"That was already there.\" \"Our tunnel cannot do that.\" \"You should have folded your mirror.\" Even when true, each one turns a claim into a fight.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"A customer says the wash broke his antenna. Which response is correct?","o":["\"Our tunnel cannot do that.\"","\"That was already loose.\"","\"I am sorry this happened on your visit. Let me get this documented properly.\"","\"You should have taken it off.\""],"a":2,"why":"Acknowledge the experience, never admit or deny fault, and move to documentation."},{"q":"Who decides a damage claim?","o":["The Tunnel Captain on the spot","The customer","Corporate, after the GM reviews it","The MSA"],"a":2,"why":"You document and escalate. Corporate decides."},{"q":"What goes in the documentation?","o":["Photos of the damage only","Photos of the damage, panel, and vehicle; plate, time, package; camera footage; incident form; Notes","A verbal description to the GM","The customer's statement only"],"a":1,"why":"Full documentation lets the claim be decided fairly. Memory and one photo do not."},{"q":"When does the claim get escalated?","o":["At the weekly meeting","Same day, to the Supervisor or GM","When the customer calls back","Never; handle it on site"],"a":1,"why":"Same day. The customer was promised a next step and the footage is still fresh."}]},{"key":"t6","title":"Leading the Crew","minutes":4,"why":"The Captain is usually the first leadership role at Mighty Wash. The Leadership Manual's rules apply from here up: the standard you walk past is the standard you accept, and a crew learns what is required from what you correct and what you recognize, not from what the handbook says.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">When you lead the lot, the standards are yours to hold.</p>\n<h2>Run the shift</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Assignments.</strong> Who is on passenger prep, driver prep, exit, vacuums. Rotate so everyone stays sharp and nobody is stuck.</li><li><strong>Breaks.</strong> Scheduled, covered, and taken. An uncovered lane at peak is a lost member.</li><li><strong>Pace.</strong> Set belt speed, watch the line, call for help before you need it.</li></ul>\n<h2>Hold the standard</h2>\n<ul><li>Correct in the moment, out loud, with the standard. \"Wheels every car; let's get that one.\"</li><li>Correct everyone the same way, the veteran and the new hire, in view of each other.</li><li>Recognize the standard being met, by name, today. \"Marcus, that exit check caught two rewashes. That is the job.\"</li><li>If you catch yourself thinking \"I'll let it go this once,\" that is the moment the standard resets.</li></ul>\n<h2>Hand off</h2>\n<p>When the Supervisor or manager arrives or at shift change: what happened, what is pending, who did well. Two minutes, every time.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"A veteran prepper skips the greeting while a new hire watches. What do you do?","o":["Let it go; he is reliable otherwise","Correct it in the moment, out loud, with the standard, the same way you would for the new hire","Tell the new hire not to copy him","Note it for the GM"],"a":1,"why":"Enforce evenly, in the moment, in view of each other. That is how the crew learns the real standard."},{"q":"Why recognize the standard being met, not just the misses?","o":["It is polite","If only misses get attention, the crew learns that avoiding getting caught is what matters","It is required by HR","It fills time"],"a":1,"why":"Recognition tells the crew that meeting the standard is what gets noticed."},{"q":"What does a shift handoff include?","o":["Just \"all good\"","What happened, what is pending, who did well","The chemical order","Nothing; the log covers it"],"a":1,"why":"Two minutes: what happened, what is pending, who did well."}]}]}],"scenarios":[{"t":"Streaky at 2 pm","s":"Cars were perfect at open. Now every car has a haze on the hood and a streak on the driver door in the same spot.","o":["Speed up the belt to clear the line.","Look at the exit, then check nozzles and the mitter on that side; the same-spot streak is mechanical. Fix or call it in, and slow the belt if needed.","Add more soap.","Wait until close to look."],"a":1,"why":"Same spot every car is mechanical. Haze points to rinse or drying agent. Check in order: exit, chemical, belt, friction."},{"t":"Off the roller","s":"A sedan turns slightly on the belt and the rear tire drops off the roller. The driver starts to open the door.","o":["Wave the driver to drive out.","Stop the belt, tell the driver to stay in the car, assess from outside the path, and clear it with two people before anyone restarts.","Push the car back on with two preppers.","Keep the belt running; the next roller will catch it."],"a":1,"why":"Stop first. Customer stays in. Two people clear it. Only the Captain or shift leader restarts."},{"t":"The mirror claim","s":"A customer at the exit says the wash cracked her mirror cap. You remember the loader flagging a loose cap on that car before it went in.","o":["Tell her the loader saw it was already loose.","Apologize for the experience, document fully including the loader's pre-wash note, pull footage, give her the next step, and escalate to the GM today.","Tell her to call corporate.","Replace it from petty cash."],"a":1,"why":"Never say \"that was already there,\" even when you believe it. The pre-wash note goes in the documentation; corporate decides."},{"t":"Phone on the lot","s":"You are the senior person on shift. A prepper checks his phone between cars, twice.","o":["Ignore it; you are not a Supervisor.","Correct it in the moment: \"Phones off the lot,\" the same way you would for anyone.","Text the GM.","Wait for the Supervisor tomorrow."],"a":1,"why":"When you are the senior person, you are the qualified leader. The standard is yours to hold."},{"t":"Friday chemical check","s":"Friday close. The drying agent drum will not make it through Saturday. The delivery is Monday.","o":["Dilute it to stretch through the weekend.","Call it in tonight and tell the GM now so they can source it before Saturday. Never dilute or mix.","Run Saturday without it.","Borrow a different product from the auto center."],"a":1,"why":"Low chemical is called in before it is out, and the GM is told. A Saturday without drying agent is wet cars all day."}],"assessment":[{"q":"The Tunnel Captain walks the tunnel ...","o":["at open only","every hour","when something breaks","at close"],"a":1},{"q":"The test car runs ...","o":["after open","before the first customer","at noon","weekly"],"a":1},{"q":"A drum that will not last through peak is swapped ...","o":["when it runs out","at startup","at close","tomorrow"],"a":1},{"q":"Clean on top, filmy on the lower panels points to ...","o":["dryers","chemical and dwell on the lower panels","the pay station","rinse"],"a":1},{"q":"Same streak, same spot, every car is ...","o":["chemical","mechanical: nozzle, mitter, or brush","the customer","dwell"],"a":1},{"q":"Stretching a low drum by diluting it is ...","o":["fine on weekends","never done","fine with GM approval","standard"],"a":1},{"q":"During a jam, customers ...","o":["get out at the exit","stay in their cars","walk out the entrance","help clear it"],"a":1},{"q":"A damage claim is decided by ...","o":["the Captain","the customer","corporate after GM review","the MSA"],"a":2},{"q":"\"That was already there\" is ...","o":["fine if true","never said to a customer","required for the form","said by the GM only"],"a":1},{"q":"When you are the senior person on the lot you are ...","o":["just the Captain","the qualified leader for that shift","off duty for leadership","the MSA"],"a":1},{"q":"Corrections are given ...","o":["weekly","in the moment, out loud, with the standard, to everyone the same way","by the GM","in writing only"],"a":1},{"q":"Every stop is ...","o":["forgotten","logged and reported to the GM","reported monthly","only logged if there was damage"],"a":1}]},{"key":"l4","num":4,"title":"Supervisor and MSA","short":"Supervisor / MSA","role":"Mighty Sales Associate: you sign up Members and protect the base. Supervisor: you run the shift as the qualified leader.","blurb":"W.A.S.H. in depth, MVP and Express Interiors, the pay station, retention, running a shift, coaching in the moment, and the Level 1 Maintenance Qualification: basic maintenance, IT, and facilities.","why":"Membership is 75 to 80% of the business and the MSA is where it is made. The Supervisor is the qualified leader the standards require on every shift. This level is both, because at Mighty Wash the person who can sell is the person who should be running the shift.","draft":true,"pass":80,"ack_formal":false,"ack_title":null,"ack_statement":"I have completed the Supervisor and MSA training and the Level 1 Maintenance Qualification. I can run W.A.S.H. with every eligible customer, sell MVP, run a clean sign-up, work retention, lead a shift as the qualified leader, coach in the moment, and handle basic maintenance, IT, and facilities tasks within the Level 1 scope, with lockout every time.","courses":[{"title":"Supervisor and Mighty Sales Associate","blurb":null,"note":null,"draft":false,"lessons":[{"key":"m1","title":"Membership Is the Engine","minutes":4,"why":"Retail revenue depends on the weather. Membership revenue arrives on the first of the month whether it rains or not. That is why the company target is 75 to 80% from Members, why the Sales Booth exists, and why the GM Standards call MSAs one of the most important operational levers in the business.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">You are the person in the Sales Booth. Every eligible customer is offered Membership, every time.</p>\n<h2>The numbers you work toward</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Standard</th></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Conversion rate</td><td><b>15 to 18%</b> of eligible retail customers join. 20%+ is the stretch in mature markets.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Daily floor</td><td><b>10%</b>. Under that triggers an RM review.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">MVP penetration</td><td><b>25 to 35%</b> of Members on MVP (unlimited exterior + Express Interiors).</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Monthly churn</td><td><b>7 to 9%</b>. Voluntary 3% or less; credit card 4% or less.</td></tr></table></div>\n<h2>The Sales Booth</h2>\n<p>On sites that have one, you work from the Sales Booth. It is where customers go to ask about Membership and where sign-ups happen, so it is staffed whenever the lanes are open. On a site without a booth, you work the pay lanes so every customer still meets you.</p>\n<h2>The packages</h2>\n<p>Three washes: <strong>Wonder</strong>, <strong>Super Shine</strong>, and <strong>Mighty Protector</strong>. Every package is available as a Membership, so the question is never \"do you want a Membership\" but \"which one.\" MVP adds Express Interiors to a Membership.</p>\n<h2>Eligible means everyone who is not a Member</h2>\n<p>Retail customers, lapsed Members, fleet drivers in their personal cars, the person who says \"I only come once a month.\" Everyone is offered. Nobody is pressured.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"What is the conversion rate standard?","o":["10%","15 to 18%","25 to 35%","75 to 80%"],"a":1,"why":"15 to 18%, with 20%+ as the stretch. Under 10% on any day triggers an RM review."},{"q":"Who is eligible to be offered Membership?","o":["Only customers who ask","Everyone who is not already a Member","Only customers buying Mighty Protector","Only locals"],"a":1,"why":"Every eligible customer, every time. Eligible means everyone who is not a Member."},{"q":"Which packages are available as a Membership?","o":["Only Mighty Protector","Only Wonder","All three: Wonder, Super Shine, and Mighty Protector","None; Membership is separate"],"a":2,"why":"Every package has a Membership. The question for the customer is which one, not whether."}]},{"key":"m2","title":"W.A.S.H. in Depth","minutes":7,"why":"W.A.S.H. exists so the offer is made the same way to every customer, not only when an MSA feels confident. The objections below are the same five you will hear every day. Knowing the answer before the customer asks is the difference between 10% and 18%.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Welcome, Answer, Support, Hook. Four steps, under a minute, every car.</p>\n<div class=\"wash\">\n<div><div class=\"L\">W</div><div class=\"w\">Welcome</div><p>Approach from the front so they see you. Eye contact. \"Welcome to Mighty Wash! First time with us?\"</p></div>\n<div><div class=\"L\">A</div><div class=\"w\">Answer</div><p>Know Wonder, Super Shine, and Mighty Protector, the Member price for each, and how it works cold. \"Members wash unlimited for about the price of two washes.\"</p></div>\n<div><div class=\"L\">S</div><div class=\"w\">Support</div><p>Ask how often they wash. Do the math with them. Connect it to their car and their week.</p></div>\n<div><div class=\"L\">H</div><div class=\"w\">Hook</div><p>Ask. \"Want me to get you set up today? Takes about a minute.\" Then stop talking.</p></div>\n</div>\n<h2>The five objections</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>They say</th><th>You say</th></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">\"Too expensive\"</td><td>\"Two washes a month and it has paid for itself. Most Members come every week once it is free to.\"</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">\"I don't come enough\"</td><td>\"That is the thing about unlimited. Members stop deciding whether it is worth it and just pull in. Rain, dust, pollen, whatever.\"</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">\"Let me think about it\"</td><td>\"Sure. Quick version: it is month to month and you can change or cancel. Want me to set it up now so today's wash counts?\"</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">\"I have one somewhere else\"</td><td>\"How is that going for you? We have Express Interiors on MVP and free vacuums every visit. Worth a comparison next month?\"</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">\"I'm just visiting\"</td><td>\"No problem. Enjoy the wash.\" Not every customer is eligible today. Thank them and move on.</td></tr></table></div>\n<h2>Rules</h2>\n<ul><li>Every eligible customer gets the Hook. Not most. Every.</li><li>Never pressure, never guilt, never argue. One ask, one follow-up, then thank them.</li><li>If you cannot answer a question about pricing or plans, you are not ready for the lanes alone. Ask to be retrained.</li></ul>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"A customer says \"I don't come often enough for that.\" Best response?","o":["\"Then it is probably not for you.\"","\"That is the thing about unlimited. Members stop deciding whether it is worth it and just pull in.\"","\"You should come more.\"","\"It is cheaper than you think.\""],"a":1,"why":"Reframe: unlimited removes the decision. Then ask."},{"q":"What happens after you ask for the sale?","o":["Repeat the offer","Stop talking and let them answer","List the packages again","Offer a discount"],"a":1,"why":"Ask, then stop. The silence is where the yes happens."},{"q":"A customer says \"let me think about it.\" What is the standard?","o":["Let them go","One short follow-up with the month-to-month fact and an offer to set it up now, then thank them either way","Keep pitching until they decide","Give them a flyer"],"a":1,"why":"One ask, one follow-up, then thank them. Never pressure."},{"q":"You cannot confidently answer a pricing question. What does that mean?","o":["Guess","You are not ready to work the lanes alone; ask to be retrained on W.A.S.H.","Send them to the GM","Skip the Answer step"],"a":1,"why":"Every MSA is trained on W.A.S.H. before working the lanes alone and retrained if below standard."}]},{"key":"m3","title":"Selling MVP and Express Interiors","minutes":4,"why":"A Member on the base plan is worth a monthly payment. A Member on MVP is worth more every month and visits more often, because the inside of the car is what people actually live in. MVP penetration of 25 to 35% is a company target because it is the fastest way to grow revenue without a single new customer.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">The best time to sell MVP is at sign-up. The second best time is every visit after.</p>\n<h2>At sign-up</h2>\n<p>Present MVP first, not as an add-on. \"Most Members go with MVP: unlimited exterior plus Express Interiors, so the inside gets done too. Or there is exterior-only.\" Lead with the full picture; let them step down if they want.</p>\n<h2>Existing Members</h2>\n<ul><li>Member with kids, a dog, or a work truck: \"You know MVP includes Express Interiors? I can upgrade you right here.\"</li><li>Member at the vacuums doing it themselves: that is an MVP conversation.</li><li>Retail customer buying an interior: \"You would save money on MVP after two visits.\"</li></ul>\n<h2>Deliver the interior to standard</h2>\n<p>Express Interiors are delivered to standard every time. If the interior lane is slow or sloppy, MVP churns. Watch the lane; it is your product.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"How do you present MVP at sign-up?","o":["As an add-on after they pick exterior","First, with the full picture, and let them step down","Only if they ask","Only to customers with new cars"],"a":1,"why":"Lead with MVP. People step down more readily than they step up."},{"q":"Why does MVP penetration matter to the company?","o":["It is easier to sell","It grows revenue per Member without needing a new customer","It reduces labor","It replaces retail"],"a":1,"why":"Each MVP upgrade raises revenue per Member and visit frequency. 25 to 35% is the target."},{"q":"A Member is at the vacuums cleaning their own interior. What is that?","o":["A satisfied customer","An MVP conversation","A complaint waiting to happen","Nothing"],"a":1,"why":"They value a clean interior and are doing the work themselves. Offer the upgrade."}]},{"key":"m4","title":"The Pay Station and POS","minutes":5,"why":"A sign-up done wrong at the pay station becomes a declined card, a wrong plate on the tag, or a Member who cannot get through the gate next week. Each of those is a churn event you created. Accuracy at the pay station is retention.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">The pay station is where Membership becomes real. Get the details right the first time.</p>\n<h2>A clean sign-up</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Confirm the plan (MVP or exterior) and the price out loud.</li><li>Plate or tag: match it to the vehicle in front of you. Read it back.</li><li>Payment: run it while they are there. A declined card at sign-up is handled now, not next month.</li><li>Contact info: phone and email correct. That is how failed payments get recovered.</li><li>Tell them how it works: \"Your plate is recognized at the pay station. Pull into the Member lane and go.\"</li></ol>\n<h2>Changes, cancellations, refunds</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Plan changes</strong> you can do on the spot. Upgrades especially.</li><li><strong>Cancellation requests</strong> follow the company's cancellation process. First, understand why (see Retention). Then route it correctly and put it in Notes.</li><li><strong>Refunds:</strong> direct the customer to corporate. Do not quote an amount. Ever.</li></ul>\n<h2>Notes</h2>\n<p>Every meaningful interaction, issue, and resolution goes in the Notes feature so the next person can pick up the history. If it is not in Notes, it did not happen.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"A customer's card declines at sign-up. What do you do?","o":["Sign them up and let billing sort it out","Handle it now: try another card or payment, so the account starts clean","Give them a free month","Cancel the sign-up"],"a":1,"why":"A declined card at sign-up is a churn event waiting to happen. Fix it while they are there."},{"q":"A Member asks how much their refund will be. You ...","o":["look it up and tell them","direct them to corporate and do not quote an amount","estimate it","refund it at the pay station"],"a":1,"why":"Refunds go through corporate and the amount is never quoted on site."},{"q":"Why read the plate back during sign-up?","o":["It is polite","A wrong plate means the gate will not recognize them next visit","It speeds things up","Corporate requires it"],"a":1,"why":"The plate is how the Member gets through the gate. Read it back and match it to the car."},{"q":"Where does a cancellation conversation get recorded?","o":["Nowhere","In the Notes feature","In an email","On a sticky note"],"a":1,"why":"Every meaningful interaction goes in Notes so anyone can pick up the history."}]},{"key":"m5","title":"Retention and Save Conversations","minutes":5,"why":"A lost Member takes months of new sign-ups to replace. The churn targets split into voluntary (people who chose to leave) and credit card (people who did not choose to leave and are gone anyway) because they need different fixes. Half of retention is a phone call about an expired card.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Protecting the base is as much the job as growing it.</p>\n<h2>Credit card churn (target 4% or less)</h2>\n<ul><li>A failed payment is a Member who still wants to be a Member. Call, text, or catch them at the gate the same week.</li><li>\"Hi, this is Mighty Wash. Your card on file did not go through. Takes a minute to update, want to do it now?\"</li><li>Update it at the pay station when they visit. Never let a failed card sit for a second cycle.</li></ul>\n<h2>Voluntary churn (target 3% or less)</h2>\n<p>Ask why before you process anything. Then match the save to the reason:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>Reason</th><th>Save</th></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">\"Not using it enough\"</td><td>Remind them of Express Interiors and free vacuums. Offer a step down to exterior rather than a cancel.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">\"Too expensive right now\"</td><td>Step down, or a pause if the company offers one. Keep the relationship.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">\"Moving\"</td><td>Check our locations. If none nearby, thank them and make the cancel easy. They will tell people how it ended.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">\"Bad experience\"</td><td>Listen, apologize, fix what you can today, get a leader. This is the one you can save in person.</td></tr></table></div>\n<h2>Record it</h2>\n<p>Cancellation reasons go in Notes. The GM reads them to find what is driving churn at your site.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"A Member's card failed this month. What is the standard?","o":["Wait for them to notice","Contact them the same week and update it at the pay station when they visit","Cancel the Membership","Send a letter"],"a":1,"why":"A failed card is a Member who still wants to be one. Same week, never a second cycle."},{"q":"A Member wants to cancel because they are not using it enough. First move?","o":["Process the cancellation","Ask about Express Interiors and vacuums, then offer a step down instead of a cancel","Offer a free month","Tell them to come more"],"a":1,"why":"Match the save to the reason. \"Not using it\" often means they forgot what is included."},{"q":"Why do cancellation reasons go in Notes?","o":["Corporate requires it","The GM reads them to find what is driving churn at the site","To track the MSA","They do not"],"a":1,"why":"Reasons in Notes are how the site learns what is causing churn."},{"q":"What are the two churn sub-targets?","o":["Voluntary 3% or less, credit card 4% or less","Voluntary 7%, credit card 9%","Voluntary 4%, credit card 3%","There are none"],"a":0,"why":"Voluntary 3% or less; credit card 4% or less, inside the 7 to 9% monthly target."}]},{"key":"m6","title":"Running a Shift as Supervisor","minutes":5,"why":"The GM Standards require a qualified leader on every shift, including the GM's days off. That is the Supervisor. A shift without a leader runs on whatever happens; a shift with one runs on the standard. Sundays and Mondays, when GMs are typically off, are where this matters most.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">When you are the leader on shift, the site's standards are yours to hold until the next leader arrives.</p>\n<h2>Open the shift</h2>\n<ul><li>Confirm the site opened on time and the test car ran. If either missed, report it up immediately.</li><li>Two-minute huddle: yesterday's conversion, today's focus, who is where, breaks.</li><li>Walk the lot as a customer once. Trash, hoses, signage, vacuums.</li></ul>\n<h2>Run the shift</h2>\n<ul><li>MSA coverage on every pay lane at peak. An unstaffed lane is a lost Member.</li><li>Watch the exit, the line, and the interior lane. Move people before the problem, not after.</li><li>Handle escalations: complaints, claims, stops. Bring the issue and your plan when you call the GM.</li><li>Hellowash tickets and Notes for your shift, closed out before you leave.</li></ul>\n<h2>Close the shift</h2>\n<ul><li>Nightly clean verified to the full checklist, not \"looked fine.\"</li><li>Shutdown confirmed with the Tunnel Captain. Chemical for tomorrow noted.</li><li>Handoff note to the GM: numbers, issues, who did well.</li></ul>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"Why must every shift have a qualified leader?","o":["For payroll","Without one, the shift runs on whatever happens instead of the standard","So someone can take breaks","It is optional"],"a":1,"why":"The GM Standards require a qualified leader on every shift, including GM days off."},{"q":"What is in the shift huddle?","o":["Company history","Yesterday's conversion, today's focus, who is where, breaks","The schedule for next month","Nothing; just start"],"a":1,"why":"Two minutes: yesterday's number, today's focus, assignments, breaks."},{"q":"When you call the GM about a problem, you bring ...","o":["the problem","the problem and your plan","a list of who caused it","nothing; the GM decides"],"a":1,"why":"Solve what you can; when you escalate, bring the issue and your plan."},{"q":"How is the nightly clean closed out?","o":["\"Looked fine\"","Verified against the full checklist","By the Captain only","Skipped on slow nights"],"a":1,"why":"Verified to the full checklist. The RM sees the result on camera in the morning."}]},{"key":"m7","title":"Coaching in the Moment","minutes":4,"why":"The GM Standards say MSAs are coached live on the lot: observe the pitch, give immediate feedback, recognize wins. A correction a week later is a suggestion; a correction on the spot is the standard. The Supervisor is on the lot with the team all shift, which makes you the main coach at the site.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Watch the pitch. Say what you saw. Say the standard. Recognize what was right.</p>\n<h2>Observe W.A.S.H. live</h2>\n<p>Stand where you can hear an MSA work a car. Did they Welcome from the front? Answer confidently? Support with the math? Hook with a real ask? Most misses are a skipped Hook.</p>\n<h2>Feedback in 30 seconds</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li>What you saw: \"You answered the price question well.\"</li><li>The standard: \"We ask for the sale on every eligible car.\"</li><li>The fix: \"Next car, after the math, say: want me to set you up today?\"</li><li>Watch the next one and recognize it: \"That was it. That is the Hook.\"</li></ol>\n<h2>Rules that protect the standard</h2>\n<ul><li>Enforce evenly: the top seller and the new MSA get the same correction, in view of each other.</li><li>Below standard after coaching: retrain on W.A.S.H. before they work the lanes alone again.</li><li>Recognize by name, today. The team repeats what gets noticed.</li></ul>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"What is the most common W.A.S.H. miss?","o":["Welcome","Answer","Support","The Hook: not asking for the sale"],"a":3,"why":"Most MSAs can greet and answer. Skipping the ask is the usual miss."},{"q":"Feedback is given ...","o":["at the end of the week","in the moment, in about 30 seconds, with the standard","in writing","only by the GM"],"a":1,"why":"Correct in the moment. A correction a week later reads as a suggestion."},{"q":"An MSA is still below standard after coaching. What is next?","o":["Move them to the exit","Retrain on W.A.S.H. before they work the lanes alone again","Write them up","Lower the target"],"a":1,"why":"Re-train anyone below standard before they work the lanes alone."}]}]},{"title":"Level 1 Maintenance Qualification","blurb":null,"note":null,"draft":false,"lessons":[{"key":"m8","title":"Basic Maintenance","minutes":7,"why":"Most tunnel downtime starts as something small: a plugged nozzle, a worn tip, a slipping roller, a vacuum with a torn bag. A Supervisor who can see it, fix the safe ones, and write a clear ticket for the rest keeps the site open and keeps the maintenance team working on real problems instead of driving out to clear a nozzle. The qualification exists so you know exactly what is yours to touch and what is not.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Level 1 Maintenance means you can inspect, do the safe routine tasks, and write a ticket that gets the rest fixed fast. It does not mean you work on anything powered, pressurized, or electrical.</p>\n<h2>Lockout first, every time</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li><strong>Stop</strong> the equipment at the control and tell the lot you are working on it.</li><li><strong>Disconnect</strong> at the equipment's disconnect switch, not just the control panel.</li><li><strong>Tag</strong> it with your name and the time so nobody restarts it.</li><li><strong>Verify</strong> it will not start before you put a hand on anything. Then work.</li><li><strong>Clear and restart</strong> only after your tools and hands are out and the tag is removed by you.</li></ol>\n<h2>What Level 1 can do</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Daily walk with the Tunnel Captain:</strong> listen for new noises, look for leaks, drips, loose hoses, worn cloth, and anything hanging into the vehicle path.</li><li><strong>Nozzles and tips:</strong> clear a plugged nozzle and replace a worn tip with the arch off and locked out. Match the tip to the one you removed.</li><li><strong>Vacuums:</strong> empty and clean the separator or bag, clear a blocked hose, replace a cracked hose or nozzle, check the intake screen.</li><li><strong>Dryers:</strong> clean intake screens of lint and debris. Report any rattle or vibration; do not open a blower housing.</li><li><strong>Conveyor:</strong> keep the pit and track clear of debris. Report any roller that sticks, drags, or does not drop.</li><li><strong>Chemical station:</strong> swap drums, prime lines per the vendor procedure, replace a cracked tube, keep the area clean and labeled. Gloves and eye protection.</li><li><strong>Lights and signs:</strong> replace plug-in bulbs and signage inserts you can reach from the ground or a stepladder with a spotter.</li></ul>\n<h2>What Level 1 does not do</h2>\n<ul><li>Anything inside an electrical panel, including resetting a tripped breaker more than once. One reset, and if it trips again it is a ticket.</li><li>Hydraulic lines, pumps, motors, gearboxes, air compressor internals, or anything under pressure.</li><li>Conveyor drive, chain, or roller mechanism repairs.</li><li>Working at height beyond a stepladder, or inside the tunnel with any equipment energized.</li></ul>\n<h2>Write a ticket that gets fixed</h2>\n<p>What is wrong, where exactly (which arch, which vacuum, which lane), when it started, what you already tried, and a photo. Mark it <strong>urgent</strong> if it affects customers or safety right now. Follow it until it is closed; a ticket that sits is a problem that stays.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"Before clearing a plugged nozzle on an arch, what must happen?","o":["Turn the arch off at the control panel","Stop, disconnect at the equipment, tag it, and verify it will not start","Wait for a gap between cars","Ask the Captain to slow the belt"],"a":1,"why":"Lockout is the whole sequence: stop, disconnect, tag, verify. The control panel alone is not enough."},{"q":"A breaker trips, you reset it, and it trips again. What now?","o":["Reset it again and watch it","Stop. A second trip is a ticket, not a second reset","Hold it in","Move the load to another breaker"],"a":1,"why":"One reset. If it trips again there is a fault, and that is beyond Level 1."},{"q":"Which task is within Level 1?","o":["Replacing a hydraulic hose","Opening a dryer blower housing","Replacing a cracked vacuum hose and cleaning the separator","Adjusting the conveyor drive chain"],"a":2,"why":"Vacuums, nozzles, chemical drums, screens, and reachable bulbs are Level 1. Hydraulics, drives, and blower internals are not."},{"q":"What makes a maintenance ticket useful?","o":["\"Tunnel broken\"","What, exactly where, when it started, what you tried, a photo, and the right priority","A phone call with no record","The vendor's name"],"a":1,"why":"A specific ticket gets fixed on the first visit. A vague one gets a second visit."}]},{"key":"m9","title":"Basic IT","minutes":6,"why":"When the pay station freezes or the internet drops, the site stops selling Memberships until someone fixes it, and the first person who can is whoever is on shift. Most site IT problems clear with a correct restart in the correct order. The rules on logins and links exist because one shared password or one bad click can take down more than one site.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Know the restart order, know what to check before you call, and never guess on security.</p>\n<h2>The restart order</h2>\n<p>When \"the internet is down,\" power-cycle in this order and wait a full two minutes between steps: <strong>modem → router → network switch → the device that is failing</strong> (pay station, POS terminal, camera). Restarting the pay station first, before the network, wastes ten minutes.</p>\n<h2>Pay stations and POS</h2>\n<ul><li>Frozen screen: restart the station per the site procedure. If it comes back and freezes again within the hour, open a ticket; do not keep restarting through peak.</li><li>Card reader not reading: check the reader for debris and a loose cable before anything else.</li><li>Receipt paper: keep two rolls at every station. Out of paper looks like a broken station to a customer.</li><li>Plate or tag not recognized: wipe the camera lens, check the plate is readable and the Member's account is active in the POS. Do not create a duplicate account.</li></ul>\n<h2>Cameras</h2>\n<p>A camera offline means the RM cannot verify your open, test car, and clean. Check power and the network cable at the camera and the switch, then restart in order. Still down: ticket it the same day and tell the GM, because the RM will ask.</p>\n<h2>Security rules that are not optional</h2>\n<ul><li>Logins are personal. Never share yours and never use someone else's, including the GM's.</li><li>No personal accounts, downloads, or browsing on site devices.</li><li>Lock the screen when you walk away from the office computer.</li><li>Nobody from \"IT,\" \"the bank,\" or \"corporate\" will ever call or email asking for a password, a code, or gift cards. Hang up, do not click, and report it.</li><li>Lost phone or tablet with site apps on it: report it the same hour so access can be cut.</li></ul>\n<h2>Google Workspace and Hellowash</h2>\n<p>Email answered in a timely manner, every Calendar invite accepted or declined, cameras on in Meet, and Hellowash tickets closed out promptly. These are GM Standards and they start at Supervisor.</p>\n<h2>When to call IT</h2>\n<p>After the restart order has been done once, correctly. Have ready: the site, the device, what it shows, what you tried, and since when.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"The internet is down and the pay station shows offline. What is the correct restart order?","o":["Pay station, then router, then modem","Modem, router, switch, then the pay station, two minutes between each","Switch first","Call IT before touching anything"],"a":1,"why":"Network first, from the outside in, then the device. Restarting the device before the network wastes time."},{"q":"A pay station freezes, restarts fine, then freezes again 30 minutes later. You ...","o":["keep restarting it through peak","open a ticket; repeated freezes are a fault, not a restart problem","unplug it for the day","swap it with another lane's station"],"a":1,"why":"One restart. A repeat within the hour is a ticket."},{"q":"Someone calls saying they are from corporate IT and needs your login to fix the POS. You ...","o":["give it; they sound legitimate","refuse, hang up, and report it. Nobody will ever ask for a password by phone or email","give them the GM's login instead","ask them to email you"],"a":1,"why":"No legitimate request for a password, code, or gift cards ever comes by phone or email."},{"q":"A camera is offline. Why does it matter today?","o":["It does not; cameras are for security only","The RM verifies open, test car, and nightly clean on it every morning","Only the vendor cares","Corporate reviews it monthly"],"a":1,"why":"The RM cannot verify your site without it. Check power and cable, restart in order, then ticket it the same day."}]},{"key":"m10","title":"Basic Facilities","minutes":6,"why":"A customer judges the wash before the car touches water: the entrance, the signs, the lights, the vacuum area, the restroom. Facilities are the part of the customer experience the tunnel cannot fix. The nightly clean keeps the site presentable day to day; the facilities walk catches what wears out slowly, so it is fixed before the RM's site visit or a customer's review finds it.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">The nightly clean is daily. The facilities walk is weekly. Both are checklists, not impressions.</p>\n<h2>The weekly facilities walk</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>Area</th><th>Look for</th></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Entrance and signage</td><td>Menu boards readable and current, package names correct, lights working, no faded or broken panels, directional signs and striping visible</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Lot and drive lanes</td><td>Potholes, loose concrete, standing water, drain grates clear, cones and bollards in place, trash enclosure closed and clean</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Pay stations and Sales Booth</td><td>Clean, lit, screens clear, booth stocked and presentable, no exposed wiring</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Tunnel exterior and canopy</td><td>Lights, gutters, roof leaks, loose panels, bird and wasp nests</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Vacuum area</td><td>Every station working, hoses and nozzles intact, mats, trash cans lined and emptied, lighting at night</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Restrooms</td><td>Clean, stocked, locks and fixtures working, no odor</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Landscaping and curb appeal</td><td>Mowed, trimmed, beds clear, no weeds in the lot, flags and banners in good shape</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Life safety</td><td>Fire extinguishers charged and tagged, first aid kit stocked, eyewash accessible, exits and walkways clear</td></tr></table></div>\n<h2>Seasonal</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Freeze:</strong> West Texas and eastern New Mexico freeze hard. Before the first freeze, confirm heat tape and heaters on exposed lines, know the site's freeze procedure for the tunnel and vacuums, and keep ice melt on hand for the lanes and walkways.</li><li><strong>Wind and dust:</strong> secure banners and signs, check canopy fasteners, clean dust from pay station screens and camera lenses more often.</li><li><strong>Storms:</strong> clear drains before the rain, not after. Lightning on the lot: clear it.</li></ul>\n<h2>Vendors and tickets</h2>\n<p>Landscaping, pest control, trash, and janitorial run on schedules. Know the schedule and the contact. When a vendor misses, it is a ticket with the date they missed. Anything that affects customers is reported the day it is found and followed until resolved, and the weekly list goes to the GM for the RM's maintenance review.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"How often is the facilities walk done?","o":["Nightly","Weekly, against a checklist","Monthly","When the RM is coming"],"a":1,"why":"The nightly clean is daily; the facilities walk is weekly and catches what wears out slowly."},{"q":"A menu board still shows an old package name. Which standard does that touch?","o":["None; it is cosmetic","Customer experience and sales: the first thing a customer reads has to be correct and readable","Only the brand guide","Only IT"],"a":1,"why":"The menu board is the start of the sale. Wrong or faded means a confused first-timer."},{"q":"Before the first freeze, what is confirmed?","o":["Nothing; the tunnel is heated","Heat tape and heaters on exposed lines, the site freeze procedure, and ice melt on hand","That the vacuums are off","That the RM has been told"],"a":1,"why":"A frozen line on a January morning is a late open. Prepare before the freeze."},{"q":"The landscaping vendor missed this week. You ...","o":["mow it yourself","open a ticket with the missed date and add it to the weekly list for the GM","wait and see if they come next week","cancel the vendor"],"a":1,"why":"Vendor misses are tickets with dates. The weekly list goes to the GM for the RM maintenance review."}]}]}],"scenarios":[{"t":"\"Too expensive\"","s":"A retail customer buying Mighty Protector says Membership is too expensive for her.","o":["\"Okay, enjoy your wash.\"","\"Two Mighty Protector washes a month and it has paid for itself. Most Members come weekly once it is free to. Want me to set it up so today counts?\"","\"We have a cheaper plan.\"","\"Everyone says that.\""],"a":1,"why":"Support with the math, then Hook. One ask, one follow-up, then thank her either way."},{"t":"Cancel at the pay station","s":"A Member pulls up and says he wants to cancel. The line is building behind him.","o":["Process it so the line moves.","Ask him to pull aside, ask why, match the save to the reason, and record it in Notes. Route the cancellation correctly if he still wants it.","Tell him to call corporate.","Offer a free month."],"a":1,"why":"Ask why first. Half of cancellations have a save. Whatever happens, it goes in Notes."},{"t":"Two o'clock dip","s":"Saturday, 2 pm. Both lanes are backed up, one MSA is on break, and you can see the line but no one is working it.","o":["Wait for the break to end.","Work the line yourself or move someone onto the lane now. At peak, every lane has an MSA.","Close one lane.","Text the GM."],"a":1,"why":"An unstaffed lane at peak is a lost Member. Move people before the problem, not after."},{"t":"The skipped Hook","s":"You watch your best MSA greet, answer, and do the math perfectly, then let the customer drive off without asking.","o":["Say nothing; he is your best.","Thirty seconds, in the moment: what you saw, the standard, the fix. Watch the next car and recognize it.","Mention it at the next meeting.","Ask the GM to talk to him."],"a":1,"why":"Enforce evenly and in the moment. The best MSA gets the same coaching as the newest."},{"t":"Declined card at the gate","s":"A Member's plate is not recognized at the gate. The system shows a failed payment from last week.","o":["Let him through this once.","Explain the card did not go through, update it at the pay station right now, and thank him. Put it in Notes.","Tell him to call billing.","Charge him retail today."],"a":1,"why":"A failed card is a Member who still wants to be one. Fix it now, never let it sit a second cycle."},{"t":"Frozen pay station at noon","s":"Saturday, noon. Lane 2's pay station is frozen. The line is building and the internet light on the switch looks normal.","o":["Restart the modem and router first.","Restart the pay station per the site procedure; the network looks fine so start with the device. If it freezes again within the hour, open a ticket and move the MSA to work lane 1.","Unplug it and send everyone to lane 1 for the day.","Call IT before touching it."],"a":1,"why":"If the network is up, the device is the problem. One restart; a repeat is a ticket, and the Sales Booth keeps selling either way."},{"t":"The nozzle","s":"The Tunnel Captain shows you a plugged nozzle on the presoak arch leaving a dry stripe on every car. He wants to clear it between cars.","o":["Let him; it takes ten seconds.","Stop the belt, disconnect and tag the arch, verify, then clear it. Ten seconds is not a reason to skip lockout.","Open a ticket and leave the stripe until the tech comes.","Turn the arch off at the control panel and let him reach in."],"a":1,"why":"Clearing a nozzle is Level 1 work, but only locked out. The control panel alone is not lockout."}],"assessment":[{"q":"Membership should be what share of revenue?","o":["25 to 35%","50%","75 to 80%","90%"],"a":2},{"q":"The conversion standard is ...","o":["10%","15 to 18%","25 to 35%","50%"],"a":1},{"q":"The H in W.A.S.H. is ...","o":["Help","Hook: ask for the sale, every eligible customer","Hand off","Hold"],"a":1},{"q":"After you ask for the sale, you ...","o":["repeat the offer","stop talking","list packages","offer a discount"],"a":1},{"q":"MVP is presented ...","o":["as an add-on","first, with the full picture","only on request","only to new cars"],"a":1},{"q":"A refund amount is ...","o":["quoted from the system","never quoted; direct to corporate","estimated","paid from the register"],"a":1},{"q":"A failed card is contacted ...","o":["next month","the same week","never","after two cycles"],"a":1},{"q":"Voluntary churn target is ...","o":["3% or less","4% or less","7 to 9%","10%"],"a":0},{"q":"Every shift must have ...","o":["two MSAs","a qualified leader","the GM on site","a Tunnel Captain only"],"a":1},{"q":"An MSA below standard after coaching is ...","o":["moved to the exit","retrained on W.A.S.H. before working lanes alone","written up","given a lower target"],"a":1},{"q":"When escalating to the GM, you bring ...","o":["the problem","the problem and your plan","names","nothing"],"a":1},{"q":"The most common W.A.S.H. miss is ...","o":["Welcome","Answer","Support","the Hook"],"a":3},{"q":"Lockout means ...","o":["turning it off at the control panel","stop, disconnect, tag, verify","telling the Captain","waiting for a gap"],"a":1},{"q":"A breaker that trips twice is ...","o":["reset again","a ticket","held in","ignored"],"a":1},{"q":"Internet down: restart in the order ...","o":["device, router, modem","modem, router, switch, device","switch only","call IT first"],"a":1},{"q":"Someone calls asking for your login to fix the POS. You ...","o":["give it","refuse, hang up, report it","give the GM's","email it"],"a":1},{"q":"The facilities walk is ...","o":["nightly","weekly, against a checklist","monthly","before RM visits"],"a":1}]},{"key":"l5","num":5,"title":"Assistant General Manager","short":"Assistant GM","role":"Second in command. You run the site in the GM's absence and own scheduling, daily numbers, onboarding, readiness, and administration alongside the GM.","blurb":"Scheduling and labor, daily numbers, hiring and the first two weeks, site readiness, administration, and escalations. Plus the Level 2 Maintenance Qualification: preventive maintenance, site systems, and the facilities program.","why":"The AGM is the GM in training. Every GM Standard applies to the site on the days the GM is off, and the AGM is who holds it. This level is the GM Standards from the second chair.","draft":true,"pass":80,"ack_formal":false,"ack_title":null,"ack_statement":"I have completed the Assistant General Manager training and the Level 2 Maintenance Qualification. I can run the site to the General Manager Standards in the GM's absence, build and manage a schedule, act on the daily numbers, onboard new hires through their first two weeks, keep the site ready and the administration current, escalate with a plan, run the preventive maintenance program, administer site systems correctly, and manage the facilities and compliance calendar.","courses":[{"title":"Assistant General Manager","blurb":null,"note":null,"draft":false,"lessons":[{"key":"c1","title":"Second in Command","minutes":4,"why":"The GM Standards say the site's standards do not take a day off, and they single out Sundays and Mondays because that is when GMs are typically off. The AGM is the reason that sentence can be true. A site with a strong AGM runs the same on Sunday as on Wednesday.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">The GM owns the site. On the GM's days off, you do, to the same standard.</p>\n<h2>What the AGM owns</h2>\n<ul><li>The site on the GM's days off, start to finish: opening, test car, conversion, coverage, nightly clean.</li><li>The schedule, with the GM.</li><li>Daily numbers review on the mornings you open.</li><li>Onboarding every new hire's first two weeks.</li><li>Administration: Notes, Hellowash, email, calendar, maintenance follow-up.</li></ul>\n<h2>Work as one leadership team</h2>\n<ul><li>Same standards, same corrections, same words. The team should not be able to tell which of you is on by how the site runs.</li><li>Daily sync with the GM: numbers, issues, people. Five minutes.</li><li>Know where the site stands on every GM Standard. You will be measured on them next.</li></ul>\n<h2>Your Road Map</h2>\n<p>The AGM who runs the site to GM standard is the GM candidate. Ask the GM and the RM where you stand and what it takes.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"Why do the GM Standards call out Sundays and Mondays?","o":["They are the slowest days","They are the days GMs are typically off, and standards do not take a day off","Corporate audits on those days","Fleet accounts are billed then"],"a":1,"why":"The AGM is who holds the standard on the GM's days off."},{"q":"What should the team notice about the site when the AGM is on versus the GM?","o":["A more relaxed pace","Nothing; same standards, same corrections, same words","Different priorities","Fewer MSAs on the lot"],"a":1,"why":"One leadership team, one standard."},{"q":"What is the daily sync with the GM?","o":["A weekly meeting","Five minutes on numbers, issues, and people","An email at month end","Not needed"],"a":1,"why":"Numbers, issues, people, every day, five minutes."}]},{"key":"c2","title":"Scheduling and Labor","minutes":6,"why":"Labor is the largest controllable cost and the most direct lever on sales. The GM Standards call scheduling a core competency, not a support function, because the schedule is where coverage, cost, and sales get decided every week. Too thin at peak and members drive off unsold; too heavy and the budget breaks.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">A schedule is a forecast of traffic with people assigned to it.</p>\n<h2>Build it from traffic</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Pull the last few weeks of cars by day and hour. Saturdays, after-work weekdays, and the day after rain are your peaks.</li><li>Staff the tunnel first: loader, passenger prep, exit QC, Captain. Then MSAs on every pay lane at peak. Then vacuums and interior.</li><li>A qualified leader on every shift, including the GM's days off. Write their name on the shift.</li><li>Check labor against budget. Trim the shoulders of the day before you trim peak.</li><li>Publish on time, every week, so the team can plan their lives.</li></ol>\n<h2>Manage it live</h2>\n<ul><li>Weather changes everything. Rain forecast: cut hours early. Rain cleared: call people in.</li><li>Call-outs: fill the lane before the shift starts, not during.</li><li>Overtime is a decision, not an accident. Approve it or prevent it.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"eyebrow red\">The rule</div><p><em>An unstaffed lane is a lost member. Never save labor at the pay station at peak.</em></p></div>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"In what order do you staff a shift?","o":["MSAs first, then tunnel","Tunnel crew first, then MSAs on every lane at peak, then vacuums and interior","Vacuums first","Whoever is available"],"a":1,"why":"The tunnel has to run; then every lane needs a seller at peak; then the rest."},{"q":"Labor is over budget. Where do you trim first?","o":["Peak MSA coverage","The shoulders of the day, before and after peak","The Tunnel Captain","The qualified leader"],"a":1,"why":"Trim the slow edges of the day. Never the pay lanes at peak."},{"q":"Rain is forecast for the afternoon. What do you do?","o":["Keep the schedule; it might not rain","Cut hours early and call people back if it clears","Send everyone home now","Nothing; the GM decides"],"a":1,"why":"Manage in real time when weather or volume changes."},{"q":"Why is the schedule published on time?","o":["Payroll needs it","So team members can plan; it is a GM Standard","Corporate tracks it","It is not important"],"a":1,"why":"Publish schedules on time so team members can plan."}]},{"key":"c3","title":"Daily Numbers","minutes":5,"why":"The GM Standards expect a GM to know conversion, member sales, churn, and labor every day and act without being told. The AGM has to read the same numbers, because on the mornings you open there is no one else to review yesterday's conversion with the MSA team. A number you do not look at until Friday cost you four days.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Four numbers, every morning. Then act on them that day.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>Number</th><th>What it is</th><th>What you do with it</th></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Conversion</td><td>New Members divided by eligible retail customers, yesterday</td><td>Under 15%: which MSA, which hours, coach today. Under 10%: expect an RM review; have the plan ready.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Member sales</td><td>Sign-ups and MVP upgrades by MSA</td><td>Recognize the top seller by name. Retrain below standard.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Churn</td><td>Cancels and failed payments</td><td>Failed cards get called this week. Cancel reasons from Notes go to the GM.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Labor</td><td>Hours and cost against budget and against cars washed</td><td>Adjust today's shoulders. Approve or stop overtime.</td></tr></table></div>\n<h2>Where the numbers live</h2>\n<p>The SiteWatch General Sales Report and the Flexwash accounting report. Review both. When they disagree, flag the discrepancy; do not pick the one you like.</p>\n<h2>The morning review</h2>\n<p>Yesterday's conversion is reviewed every morning and gaps are addressed with the MSA team that day. In the huddle: the number, the goal, one thing to do differently today.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"Yesterday's conversion was 12%. What happens this morning?","o":["Nothing; it is above the floor","Find which MSA and which hours, and coach it today","Wait for the weekly review","Change the target"],"a":1,"why":"Below 15% is a gap. Gaps are addressed with the MSA team the same day."},{"q":"The SiteWatch report and the Flexwash report disagree. You ...","o":["use the higher one","flag the discrepancy","use the lower one","ignore both"],"a":1,"why":"Review both and flag discrepancies. That is the standard."},{"q":"A failed payment shows in churn. What is the action?","o":["Cancel the Member","Contact them this week to update the card","Wait a cycle","Email corporate"],"a":1,"why":"Credit card churn is recovered by calling the same week."}]},{"key":"c4","title":"Hiring and the First Two Weeks","minutes":5,"why":"The Leadership Manual calls the hiring window the highest-leverage moment a leader has: a new hire holds the stated standard for about as long as it takes them to see one exception go unaddressed. The AGM runs onboarding, so the AGM decides what the new hire's version of normal is.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">What you say at hiring only survives if it matches what the new hire sees in the first two weeks.</p>\n<h2>At hiring: be specific, not aspirational</h2>\n<p>Not \"we are customer-focused.\" Instead: \"Every associate greets customers within the first 10 feet of the lot, every shift. No phones on the lot. Every car gets an exit check.\" Say the standards that will be enforced, in the words you will use to enforce them.</p>\n<h2>Days 1 to 3: show</h2>\n<ul><li>Pair them with your most consistent performer, not whoever is free.</li><li>Level 1 Academy lessons completed before the first solo shift.</li><li>You check in at the end of each shift: what did you see enforced today?</li></ul>\n<h2>Days 4 to 14: hold</h2>\n<ul><li>Correct their first miss in the moment, out loud, with the standard. That first correction teaches more than the interview did.</li><li>Recognize their first standard met, by name, in front of the team.</li><li>MSA hires: W.A.S.H. trained and observed before they work the lanes alone.</li></ul>\n<h2>The exception rule</h2>\n<p>If a new hire sees a veteran skip a standard and nothing happens, your hiring conversation is gone. Enforce evenly, especially in week one.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"Which hiring statement will survive the first two weeks?","o":["\"We are a customer-first team\"","\"Every associate greets customers within the first 10 feet, every shift\"","\"We expect a lot here\"","\"Just do your best\""],"a":1,"why":"Specific and observable. It can be seen met or missed and corrected."},{"q":"Who does a new hire shadow in the first shifts?","o":["Whoever is free","Your most consistent performer","The newest employee","The GM"],"a":1,"why":"Their model of normal is built from whoever they stand next to."},{"q":"A new MSA has watched W.A.S.H. twice. Can they work the lanes alone?","o":["Yes","Not until they are trained and observed doing it to standard","Yes, if it is busy","Yes, with a script"],"a":1,"why":"Every new MSA is trained on W.A.S.H. before working the lanes alone."},{"q":"What ends a hiring conversation's effect fastest?","o":["A long first week","Seeing a veteran skip the standard with no correction","Too much training","A slow day"],"a":1,"why":"One exception unaddressed resets the new hire's standard."}]},{"key":"c5","title":"Site Readiness and Maintenance","minutes":4,"why":"On-time open, test car, and nightly clean are checked by the RM on camera every morning because they are the three things that fail quietly and cost immediately. Maintenance follow-up is in the standards because a broken vacuum reported once and forgotten is the same as never reported.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Verify, do not assume. Then follow every issue until it is closed.</p>\n<h2>Every morning you open</h2>\n<ul><li>Doors open on time. If not, report to leadership immediately, before the RM sees it on camera.</li><li>Test car ran before the first customer, inspected at the exit.</li><li>Nightly clean verified against the checklist, not \"looks fine.\"</li></ul>\n<h2>Maintenance</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Anything affecting the customer experience is reported the day it is found: vacuums, pay stations, lights, signs, gates, tunnel equipment.</li><li>One list, one owner. Each item has a date reported, who is fixing it, and a status.</li><li>Follow until resolved. Check the list daily. Chase the vendor. Tell the GM what is still open and for how long.</li><li>Maintenance priorities are reviewed with the RM weekly. Bring the list.</li></ol>\n<h2>Walk the lot as a customer</h2>\n<p>Once a shift, from the entrance to the exit, through the vacuums. What would a first-timer notice? Fix it or list it.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"How do you confirm the nightly clean happened?","o":["Ask the closer","Verify against the full checklist","Assume it did","Check the camera at noon"],"a":1,"why":"Verify against the checklist. The RM is verifying on camera; so should you."},{"q":"A maintenance item was reported last week and is still open. What is the standard?","o":["It was reported; that is enough","Follow it until resolved: chase the vendor and tell the GM how long it has been open","Report it again next month","Remove it from the list"],"a":1,"why":"Reported and forgotten is the same as never reported. Follow until resolved."},{"q":"How often are maintenance priorities reviewed with the RM?","o":["Daily","Weekly, at the site visit","Quarterly","Never"],"a":1,"why":"Weekly site visits cover maintenance priorities."}]},{"key":"c6","title":"Administration","minutes":4,"why":"A multi-site company runs on the written record and the calendar. The admin standards exist because every one of them is a place where a customer, a vendor, or a colleague is waiting on the site: a Hellowash ticket is a customer who already called, an unanswered invite is a meeting that does not happen, and a missing Note is a history nobody can pick up.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">The same administration standards the GM is reviewed on. You share them.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>Area</th><th>Standard</th></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Notes feature</td><td>Every meaningful customer interaction, issue, and resolution, so anyone can pick up the history.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Hellowash tickets</td><td>Review and close out tickets from the AI answering service for your site promptly. Each one is a customer who already reached out.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Email and calendar</td><td>Respond in a timely manner. Accept or decline every Google Calendar invite.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Virtual meetings</td><td>Cameras on for every Google Meet call.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Fleet accounts</td><td>Contact and support Fleet accounts using the company's established process. Keep account information current.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Refunds</td><td>Direct customers to corporate. Do not quote the refund amount.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Sales reporting</td><td>Review SiteWatch General Sales Report and Flexwash accounting report. Flag discrepancies.</td></tr></table></div>\n<h2>Make it a routine</h2>\n<p>Fifteen minutes at open and fifteen at close: Hellowash, email, calendar, Notes from the shift, maintenance list. Done daily, admin is small. Done weekly, it is a Saturday.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"What is a Hellowash ticket?","o":["A maintenance request","A customer contact from the AI answering service that needs review and close-out","A fleet invoice","An RM note"],"a":1,"why":"Each ticket is a customer who already reached out. Close them promptly."},{"q":"A Google Calendar invite arrives. The standard is ...","o":["respond if you can attend","accept or decline every invite","ignore it if busy","forward to the GM"],"a":1,"why":"Accept or decline every invite. Respond to email in a timely manner."},{"q":"Why do interactions go in Notes?","o":["Corporate audits it","So anyone can pick up the history without the customer re-explaining","To track employees","They do not need to"],"a":1,"why":"Notes let the next person pick up where the last one left off."}]},{"key":"c7","title":"Escalations and Judgment","minutes":4,"why":"The GM Standards say: solve problems before they reach your Regional Manager, and when you escalate, bring the issue and your plan. The AGM makes the same judgment one level down. Most of what reaches a GM on their day off should have been handled, and what does reach them should arrive with a recommendation.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Handle what you can. Escalate what you must. Always with a plan.</p>\n<h2>Decide at your level</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Customer complaints:</strong> listen, apologize, fix it today if it is within your authority (rewash, interior redo, a Note and a follow-up call).</li><li><strong>Damage claims:</strong> documented by the Captain, reviewed by you the same day, sent up for the corporate decision. You do not promise an outcome.</li><li><strong>Refunds:</strong> corporate. No amount quoted.</li><li><strong>Staffing:</strong> call-outs, swaps, and sending people home are yours.</li><li><strong>Conduct:</strong> correct in the moment. Repeated misses: document and tell the GM.</li></ul>\n<h2>Escalate to the GM when</h2>\n<ul><li>A standard is being missed repeatedly by the same person.</li><li>A claim, injury, or incident involves police, an ambulance, or a threat.</li><li>Equipment is down with no fix in sight.</li><li>A decision will cost money beyond your authority.</li></ul>\n<h2>How to escalate</h2>\n<p>\"Here is what happened. Here is what I have done. Here is what I recommend. Do you agree?\" One message. The GM can say yes from their phone.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"Which of these do you handle without escalating?","o":["An injury requiring an ambulance","A rewash and a follow-up call for a complaint","A refund amount","A repeated conduct problem"],"a":1,"why":"Complaints you can fix today are yours. Injuries, refunds, and repeated misses go up."},{"q":"What does a good escalation message contain?","o":["The problem","What happened, what you did, what you recommend","Who is to blame","A request for the GM to come in"],"a":1,"why":"Bring the issue and your plan. The GM can answer from their phone."},{"q":"A damage claim is reviewed by the AGM and then ...","o":["decided by the AGM","sent up for the corporate decision, same day","paid from petty cash","closed if the customer does not call back"],"a":1,"why":"Document, review same day, send up. Corporate decides."}]}]},{"title":"Level 2 Maintenance Qualification","blurb":null,"note":null,"draft":true,"lessons":[{"key":"c8","title":"Preventive Maintenance and Equipment Faults","minutes":7,"why":"Level 1 fixes what is already broken. Level 2 keeps it from breaking. A tunnel that is maintained on a schedule fails on a Tuesday morning when a tech can come; a tunnel that is run until it breaks fails on a Saturday at 2 pm. The AGM owns the schedule, reads the faults, and decides when a reset is enough and when it is a call.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Level 2 means you run the preventive maintenance program, read equipment faults, verify the crew's lockouts, and manage the vendor when it is beyond the site.</p>\n<h2>The PM schedule</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>Frequency</th><th>What gets done</th></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Daily</td><td>Captain's startup and shutdown checklist, test car, chemical levels, nozzle and spray pattern check, pit and track clear</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Weekly</td><td>Conveyor walk: roller and chain wear, guide rail, drive sprocket. Cloth and brush condition and alignment. Dryer intake screens. Air compressor drained, filters checked. Reclaim pit level and screens. Vacuum separators and motors.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Monthly</td><td>Grease points per the equipment manual. Hydraulic power pack fluid level and leaks. Water softener or RO salt and filters. Chemical tip and flow calibration with the vendor. Belt and hose inspection on every arch. Lighting audit.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Quarterly and seasonal</td><td>Cloth and brush replacement per wear, not per calendar. Dryer motor and bearing check by the vendor. Freeze prep before the first freeze. Full pit clean-out.</td></tr></table></div>\n<p>The schedule lives in one place, each task has a name and a date, and completed tasks are logged. A PM that is not written down did not happen.</p>\n<h2>Reading faults</h2>\n<ul><li>The controller or PLC shows a fault code when something stops. Read it, write it down with the time, then look at the equipment it names before resetting anything.</li><li><strong>One reset.</strong> If the same fault returns within the shift, stop resetting and call it in with the code and what you observed. Repeated resets hide a failing part until it fails for good.</li><li>Learn the five or six faults your tunnel shows most. Keep a card at the controller: code, likely cause, what to check, when to call.</li></ul>\n<h2>Verify the crew's lockouts</h2>\n<p>A Level 1 clears a nozzle; a Level 2 confirms the disconnect is off and the tag is on before they reach in, and corrects it in the moment when it is not. The standard you walk past is the one the crew learns, and this one can hurt someone.</p>\n<h2>Vendors and parts</h2>\n<ul><li>Keep a critical spares list on site: nozzle tips, hoses, belts, fuses, vacuum hoses and nozzles, cloth sections. Reorder at the minimum, not at zero.</li><li>When you call a tech: fault code, what you observed, what you tried, since when, and a photo. Ask for the arrival window and tell the GM.</li><li>Log downtime: start, end, cause, fix, cost. The GM and RM manage the budget from this log.</li></ul>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"A fault code appears and clears when you reset it. It comes back 40 minutes later. You ...","o":["reset it again; it cleared once","stop resetting, call it in with the code and what you observed","ignore it until close","swap the fuse"],"a":1,"why":"One reset. A returning fault is a failing part. Repeated resets hide it until it fails for good."},{"q":"Cloth and brushes are replaced ...","o":["every quarter on the calendar","per wear, checked weekly","when a customer complains","when the vendor says"],"a":1,"why":"Replacement is by condition. The weekly walk is where wear gets seen."},{"q":"What makes a PM task count as done?","o":["The Captain said so","It is logged with a name and a date","The tunnel is running","The vendor was called"],"a":1,"why":"A PM that is not written down did not happen. The log is how the GM and RM know the program is real."},{"q":"A Level 1 is about to clear a nozzle with the arch turned off at the control panel only. As Level 2 you ...","o":["let them; it is quick","stop them, confirm the disconnect and tag, then let them proceed","do it yourself","report them to the GM"],"a":1,"why":"Level 2 verifies the crew's lockouts in the moment. The control panel alone is not lockout."}]},{"key":"c9","title":"Site Systems and POS Administration","minutes":7,"why":"The AGM is usually the person who adds a new hire to the POS, pulls camera footage for a claim, and notices the end-of-day report does not match the deposit. Each of those is a place where a small mistake costs real money or a real claim. Knowing the site's systems as a map, not a mystery, is what lets you fix the small things and escalate the right things.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Know how the site's systems connect, administer the POS correctly, and keep the camera and user records clean.</p>\n<h2>The site network, as a map</h2>\n<p>Internet comes in through the <strong>modem</strong>, to the <strong>router</strong>, to the <strong>switch</strong>. From the switch: pay stations, the Sales Booth terminal, the office computer, the camera recorder and cameras, the plate reader, and site Wi-Fi. Keep a one-page diagram with the ISP account number and support line, the POS vendor support line, and the camera vendor on it, taped inside the network cabinet. When something is down, the map tells you where to look.</p>\n<h2>POS administration (FlexWash)</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Users:</strong> every employee has their own login with the permission level for their role. Added on day one, removed the day they leave. No shared logins, no generic accounts.</li><li><strong>Prices and packages:</strong> you do not change a price, a package, or a Membership plan without the GM's approval and the company's process. A wrong price on the pay station is live at every lane within seconds.</li><li><strong>End of day:</strong> run the daily report, compare it to the SiteWatch General Sales Report, and investigate any difference the same day. Document what you found.</li><li><strong>Member accounts:</strong> no duplicate accounts; fix the plate on the existing one. Failed payments worked weekly from the report, not from memory.</li></ul>\n<h2>Cameras and footage</h2>\n<ul><li>Know the recorder's retention window. Footage for a claim is exported and saved to the claim file the same day, before it rolls off.</li><li>Export the load, the tunnel, and the exit for the vehicle in question with the time stamp visible. Note the camera names.</li><li>A camera down is reported the same day. The RM's morning check depends on it.</li></ul>\n<h2>Security and incidents</h2>\n<ul><li>Offboarding is same day: POS, email, camera app, any site app. Keep a checklist.</li><li>Anything that looks like a breach (locked files, ransom message, accounts you did not create, a device you do not recognize on the network) is reported to the GM and IT immediately. Do not try to fix it. Do not pay anything.</li><li>Nobody writes down a card number. Ever. Card data stays in the terminal.</li></ul>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"A new MSA starts Monday. What is the POS rule?","o":["Share the Supervisor's login until they are trained","Their own login with their role's permissions, created on day one, removed the day they leave","Use a generic \"MSA\" account","No login until 30 days"],"a":1,"why":"Personal logins, correct permissions, same-day add and remove."},{"q":"A Member asks you to drop the Super Shine Membership price for them at the pay station. You ...","o":["change it; it is one customer","do not change prices or plans without the GM's approval and the company process","change it and tell the GM later","ask corporate by email and change it meanwhile"],"a":1,"why":"Price and package changes go live at every lane. They follow the process, every time."},{"q":"A damage claim comes in on Tuesday for a wash on Saturday. First IT step?","o":["Wait for the GM","Export the footage today, before it rolls off the recorder, and save it to the claim file","Ask the customer for photos","Restart the recorder"],"a":1,"why":"Retention windows are short. Export the same day with time stamps visible."},{"q":"The office computer shows a message demanding payment to unlock files. You ...","o":["pay it; the site needs the computer","report it to the GM and IT immediately, touch nothing, pay nothing","restart it","delete the message and keep working"],"a":1,"why":"That is a security incident. Report immediately, do not try to fix it, never pay."}]},{"key":"c10","title":"Facilities Program and Compliance Basics","minutes":6,"why":"At Level 1 you walk the site and report. At Level 2 you run the program: the vendors show up because you manage them, the inspections happen because you track them, and the repair budget lasts the year because you know what it is. A missed backflow test or an expired extinguisher tag is a fine and a closed site; a vendor who misses three weeks is a site that looks abandoned.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Own the facilities calendar, the vendors, and the small-spend decisions, and know the compliance items that can close a site.</p>\n<h2>The monthly facilities inspection</h2>\n<p>The weekly walk looks for what is wrong. The monthly inspection scores every area against the same checklist, with photos, so the GM and RM can see the trend. Lighting, pavement, signage, canopy, vacuums, restrooms, landscaping, trash, life safety. Score it, date it, file it.</p>\n<h2>Vendor management</h2>\n<ul><li>One list: every vendor, what they do, how often, the contact, the contract end date, and whether they are on schedule this month.</li><li>Misses get a dated note and a call the same week. Three misses is a conversation with the GM about replacing them.</li><li>Any contractor on site: insurance certificate on file, checked in with you, told where they may and may not work, and nobody inside the tunnel with equipment energized.</li></ul>\n<h2>Spending</h2>\n<ul><li>Know the site's repair and maintenance budget and where it stands this month.</li><li>Know your spending authority. Under it: fix it and log it. Over it: two quotes and the GM's approval. Emergency and safety: fix it, then tell the GM the same day.</li><li>Repair keeps it running; replacement is a capital request through the GM. Do not patch a failing item three times without flagging it.</li></ul>\n<h2>Compliance items that can close the site</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>Item</th><th>What you track</th></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Fire extinguishers</td><td>Monthly visual check initialed on the tag; annual inspection by the vendor</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Backflow preventer</td><td>Annual test by a certified tester; report filed with the water utility</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Chemical storage</td><td>SDS binder current and accessible; secondary containment; labels; eyewash tested and clear</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Water discharge</td><td>Reclaim and discharge per the site's permit; no chemical to the storm drain, ever</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Injuries</td><td>Every injury reported the same shift, documented, and sent up; serious injuries escalated immediately</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Restrooms and signage</td><td>Health and sign permits current where the city requires them</td></tr></table></div>\n<p>Put every one of these on a calendar with a 30-day warning. The GM reviews the calendar monthly.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"A repair will cost more than your spending authority and it is not a safety issue. You ...","o":["fix it and log it","get two quotes and the GM's approval","wait for the quarterly meeting","ask the vendor to bill it in parts"],"a":1,"why":"Under your authority: fix and log. Over it: two quotes and the GM. Safety: fix, then tell the GM same day."},{"q":"The backflow preventer test is ...","o":["optional","annual, by a certified tester, with the report filed with the water utility","done by the Captain","done when the tunnel is replaced"],"a":1,"why":"A missed backflow test can close the site. It is on the compliance calendar with a 30-day warning."},{"q":"A contractor arrives to work on the dryers while the tunnel is running. You ...","o":["let them start; they are the experts","check their insurance certificate is on file, check them in, and make sure nothing is energized where they work","ask the Captain to slow the belt","leave them to it"],"a":1,"why":"Contractors follow the site's rules: certificate on file, checked in, no energized equipment where they work."},{"q":"The same vacuum motor has been patched three times in two months. You ...","o":["patch it again","flag it to the GM as a replacement, not a repair","take the vacuum out of service permanently","ask the vendor for a discount"],"a":1,"why":"Repair keeps it running; the third patch is a capital conversation."}]}]}],"scenarios":[{"t":"Saturday versus budget","s":"Labor is tracking 6% over budget this week. Saturday's schedule has two MSAs on each pay lane for the afternoon peak.","o":["Cut one MSA from each lane Saturday afternoon.","Keep peak coverage and trim the weekday shoulders and Saturday open and close instead.","Cut the Tunnel Captain's hours.","Ask the GM to raise the budget."],"a":1,"why":"Trim the shoulders, never the pay lanes at peak. An unstaffed lane is a lost Member."},{"t":"Sunday call-out","s":"Sunday morning, the GM's day off. The scheduled Supervisor calls out an hour before open. You are at home.","o":["Let the Tunnel Captain run it and check in by text.","Fill the leader role: come in yourself or confirm a qualified leader is on before open. Report the call-out to the GM in the daily sync.","Open late with whoever shows up.","Close for the morning."],"a":1,"why":"Every shift has a qualified leader, especially Sundays. The site's standards do not take a day off."},{"t":"Nine percent","s":"You open Monday and yesterday's conversion was 9%.","o":["Note it and watch the week.","Pull the MSA and hourly breakdown, address it with the MSA team in the huddle, coach live today, and have a plan ready for the RM review the floor triggers.","Change Sunday's target.","Wait for the GM on Tuesday."],"a":1,"why":"Gaps are addressed with the MSA team the same day. Under 10% triggers an RM review; bring the plan."},{"t":"Tickets piling up","s":"There are eleven open Hellowash tickets for your site, some a week old.","o":["They are mostly simple; leave them for the GM.","Work them today, starting with the oldest. Each is a customer who already reached out. Then make the open-and-close routine so it never stacks again.","Close them all without reading.","Ask corporate to turn off Hellowash."],"a":1,"why":"Tickets are reviewed and closed promptly. Build the daily routine so it stays small."},{"t":"The wrong trainer","s":"A Supervisor paired the new QC hire with a tenured prepper who cuts corners, because he was free.","o":["Leave it; the Supervisor made the call.","Move the new hire to your most consistent performer today and explain the standard to the Supervisor: new hires pair with the best, not the available.","Correct the tenured prepper in front of the new hire.","Wait for the 30-day review."],"a":1,"why":"The new hire's model of normal is being built right now. Pair them with the most consistent performer and teach the Supervisor why."},{"t":"The returning fault","s":"The conveyor throws the same fault code three times in one afternoon. The Captain has reset it each time and the tunnel runs for about an hour between faults.","o":["Keep resetting; it runs between faults.","Stop the resets, log the code and times, call the vendor with the details, and tell the GM. Plan for the belt to be down if it fails for good.","Slow the belt and hope.","Wait until Monday to call."],"a":1,"why":"One reset. A returning fault is a failing part, and repeated resets hide it until it fails on a Saturday."},{"t":"The $140 difference","s":"End of day: the FlexWash report and the SiteWatch report differ by $140. The Supervisor offers to adjust the entry so they match.","o":["Let them; it is small.","Investigate the cause today, document it, and flag it. Discrepancies are explained, not adjusted away.","Ignore anything under $200.","Email corporate and move on."],"a":1,"why":"Review both reports and flag discrepancies. Adjusting one to match hides the cause."}],"assessment":[{"q":"Sundays and Mondays are called out because ...","o":["they are slow","GMs are typically off and standards do not take a day off","corporate audits","fleet billing"],"a":1},{"q":"Shifts are staffed in this order ...","o":["MSAs, tunnel, vacuums","tunnel crew, MSAs on every lane at peak, then vacuums and interior","vacuums first","whoever is available"],"a":1},{"q":"Over budget, trim ...","o":["peak lanes","the shoulders of the day","the Captain","the leader"],"a":1},{"q":"Schedules are published ...","o":["when ready","on time, every week","monthly","by corporate"],"a":1},{"q":"The four daily numbers are ...","o":["cars, cash, hours, tickets","conversion, member sales, churn, labor","revenue, cost, profit, churn","opens, test cars, cleans, audits"],"a":1},{"q":"Two reports disagree. You ...","o":["use the higher","flag the discrepancy","use the lower","ignore it"],"a":1},{"q":"A new hire shadows ...","o":["whoever is free","your most consistent performer","the newest","the GM"],"a":1},{"q":"A new MSA works lanes alone ...","o":["on day one","after W.A.S.H. training and observation","after a week","never"],"a":1},{"q":"A maintenance item is ...","o":["reported once","followed until resolved","the vendor's problem","reviewed quarterly"],"a":1},{"q":"A Hellowash ticket is ...","o":["a maintenance note","a customer who already reached out","a fleet invoice","spam"],"a":1},{"q":"A good escalation contains ...","o":["the problem","what happened, what you did, what you recommend","blame","a request to come in"],"a":1},{"q":"Refund amounts are ...","o":["quoted by the AGM","never quoted; corporate","estimated","paid on site"],"a":1},{"q":"A fault code that returns after one reset is ...","o":["reset again","called in with the code and observations","ignored","a Captain problem"],"a":1},{"q":"Cloth and brushes are replaced ...","o":["quarterly","per wear","on complaint","never"],"a":1},{"q":"Price changes at the pay station require ...","o":["a Supervisor","the GM's approval and the company process","a customer request","nothing"],"a":1},{"q":"Over your spending authority and not a safety issue, you ...","o":["fix it","get two quotes and the GM's approval","wait","split the invoice"],"a":1},{"q":"A contractor works on site only with ...","o":["a business card","an insurance certificate on file and a check-in","the Captain's okay","nothing"],"a":1}]},{"key":"l6","num":6,"title":"General Manager","short":"General Manager","role":"The General Manager owns the site: its results, its appearance, its customers, and its team.","blurb":"The General Manager Standards and the Leadership Manual, Setting and Holding the Standard.","why":"The GM Standards say what good looks like. The Leadership Manual explains why standards slip and how to hold them. One is the what, the other is the how, and the why sits with each lesson so GMs act on the reasons, not just the rules.","draft":false,"pass":80,"ack_formal":true,"ack_title":"General Manager Standards","ack_statement":"I have read and understand the Mighty Wash General Manager Standards and agree to meet them at my site.","courses":[{"title":"General Manager Standards","blurb":"What good looks like at a Mighty Wash site, how your performance is measured, and how your bonus is earned.","note":"Issued by the VP of Customer Experience and Field Leadership, October 2026","draft":false,"lessons":[{"key":"a1","title":"The Role of the General Manager","minutes":4,"why":"Mighty Wash runs on the GM, not on the Regional Manager. The RM covers several sites and sees yours mostly through cameras and reports, so the person who can change tomorrow's result is you. When the GM knows the numbers daily and fixes problems early, issues stay small and the RM's time goes to coaching instead of firefighting. When the GM waits to be told, every problem costs a day or more before anyone acts. Ownership is the first standard because results are made on the lot, and that is where they have to be driven from.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">The General Manager owns the site. Every result at your wash is your responsibility: the way it looks, the way customers are treated, the number of members you sign, and the way your team shows up.</p>\n<p>These standards define what good looks like at Mighty Wash and how your performance, and your bonus, will be measured. The goal is bigger than getting cars clean. Mighty Wash is building a customer experience people choose to come back to, and a membership business that drives the company forward. The GM sets that tone every day.</p>\n<h2>What ownership means</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Treat the site like your own business.</strong> Its performance, appearance, and reputation are yours.</li>\n<li><strong>Know your numbers daily.</strong> Conversion, member sales, churn, and labor. Act on them without waiting to be told.</li>\n<li><strong>Solve problems before they reach your Regional Manager.</strong> When you do escalate, bring the issue and your plan.</li>\n<li><strong>Lead by example</strong> on professionalism, customer focus, and policy. Your team will mirror your standards.</li>\n<li><strong>Own your site seven days a week,</strong> including the days you are off.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"eyebrow red\">The standard in one line</div><p><em>Every result at your wash is yours. Know the numbers, fix problems early, and bring a plan when you escalate.</em></p></div>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"You need to escalate a problem to your Regional Manager. What do you bring?","o":["The issue, so the RM can decide what to do","The issue and your plan for solving it","A list of the team members involved","Nothing until the RM asks for details"],"a":1,"why":"The standard is to solve problems before they reach your RM, and when you do escalate, bring the issue and your plan."},{"q":"Which set of numbers is a GM expected to know every day?","o":["Total revenue only","Labor hours only","Conversion, member sales, churn, and labor","Whatever the weekly report shows"],"a":2,"why":"GMs know conversion, member sales, churn, and labor daily and act on them without being told."},{"q":"When does site ownership apply?","o":["Only on the shifts you are scheduled","Seven days a week, including your days off","Monday through Saturday","Only during peak hours"],"a":1,"why":"You own your site seven days a week, including the days you are off. Sundays and Mondays get their own standard in the Administration lesson."}]},{"key":"a2","title":"Customer Experience and Professionalism","minutes":5,"why":"Membership is a repeat decision. Every member decides each month whether Mighty Wash is worth keeping, and that decision is made at the pay station, the vacuums, and the tunnel exit, not at corporate. A manager on the lot catches a problem before it becomes a cancellation. The conduct rules matter for the same reason: a phone in hand or vaping in view tells a customer nobody is paying attention, and tells the team the standard is optional. Site readiness is non-negotiable because the first car of the day sets the tone for every car after it, and because it is the easiest thing for the RM to verify on camera.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Every customer should feel like the #1 customer of the day. Management is expected to be visible, approachable, and active on the lot, not behind a desk or a phone.</p>\n<h2>On the lot</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Greet customers all over the lot: pay stations, vacuums, tunnel exit, and interior lanes.</li>\n<li>Engage personally with members and first-time visitors. Resolve concerns on the spot whenever possible.</li>\n<li>Express Interiors are delivered to standard every time. Inspect quality and speed throughout the day.</li>\n<li>Every MSA works from the Sales Booth where the site is equipped, so customers know where to go to talk about Membership.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Professional conduct (all management)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dress code.</strong> Follow the Mighty Wash dress code at all times and enforce it for your team.</li>\n<li><strong>No cell phones.</strong> No personal cell phone use while on the lot or in front of customers.</li>\n<li><strong>No vaping.</strong> No vaping on the lot or in view of customers.</li>\n<li><strong>Respect.</strong> Speak to customers and team members with respect. No side conversations or behavior that distracts from service.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Site readiness, every day</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Site opens on time, every day. Late openings are reported to leadership immediately.</li>\n<li>A test car is run through the wash before the first customer.</li>\n<li>A thorough cleaning of the site is completed the night before so the site opens customer-ready.</li>\n<li>Maintenance issues affecting customer experience are reported promptly and followed until resolved.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"callout blue\"><div class=\"eyebrow blue\">How it is verified</div><p>Regional Managers verify opening time, the test car, and the nightly clean by camera each morning.</p></div>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"Who verifies the opening time, test car, and nightly clean, and how?","o":["The GM, in a daily self-report","The Regional Manager, by camera each morning","Corporate, through the sales report","The opening MSA, on a checklist"],"a":1,"why":"Regional Managers verify all three by camera each morning."},{"q":"Which of these is a conduct rule for all management?","o":["MSAs may work from anywhere on the lot instead of the Sales Booth","No personal cell phone use on the lot or in front of customers","Vaping is allowed behind the tunnel","Dress code applies only to MSAs"],"a":1,"why":"No personal cell phone use while on the lot or in front of customers. Dress code and no vaping apply to everyone too."},{"q":"What must happen before the first customer of the day?","o":["The schedule is posted","A test car is run through the wash","The sales report is reviewed","The Sales Booth is opened"],"a":1,"why":"A test car runs through the wash before the first customer, and the site was cleaned the night before so it opens customer-ready."},{"q":"Where does the MSA work on a site that has one?","o":["The tunnel exit","The Sales Booth, so customers know where to go for Membership","The vacuum area","The GM office"],"a":1,"why":"The Sales Booth is where customers find the MSA and where Memberships get sold."}]},{"key":"a3","title":"Sales and Conversion","minutes":7,"why":"Retail washes are one-time revenue that rises and falls with the weather. Membership is predictable monthly revenue that funds payroll, equipment, and growth, which is why 75 to 80% is the target. Every number in this lesson protects that base: W.A.S.H. so the offer is made to every eligible customer, the 10% floor so a bad day gets attention the same day instead of at month end, MVP because interiors raise revenue per member, and the churn sub-targets because a lost member costs far more to replace than to keep. The GM owns this because MSAs sell the way they are trained and coached, and nobody else is on the lot to do it.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Membership is the engine of Mighty Wash. The target is for membership to make up 75 to 80% of company revenue. MSAs are one of the most important operational levers, and the GM is responsible for hiring, training, and coaching them to sell.</p>\n<h2>The W.A.S.H. sales framework</h2>\n<div class=\"wash\">\n<div><div class=\"L\">W</div><div class=\"w\">Welcome</div><p>Greet every vehicle warmly and make eye contact. First impressions set up the sale.</p></div>\n<div><div class=\"L\">A</div><div class=\"w\">Answer</div><p>Answer questions clearly and confidently on pricing, plans, and how membership works.</p></div>\n<div><div class=\"L\">S</div><div class=\"w\">Support</div><p>Show the customer how membership fits their needs and saves them money.</p></div>\n<div><div class=\"L\">H</div><div class=\"w\">Hook</div><p>Ask for the sale. Every eligible customer is offered a membership, every time.</p></div>\n</div>\n<h2>Sales targets</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Metric</th><th>Standard</th><th>Notes</th></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Conversion rate</td><td class=\"v\">15 to 18%</td><td>20%+ stretch goal in mature markets</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Daily conversion floor</td><td class=\"v\">10%</td><td>Any day under 10% triggers an RM review and a conversation with leadership</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">MVP penetration</td><td class=\"v\">25 to 35%</td><td>Of member base. MVP = unlimited exterior + Express Interiors</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Monthly churn</td><td class=\"v\">7 to 9%</td><td>Long-term target. Voluntary churn 3% or less; credit card churn 4% or less</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Membership revenue mix</td><td class=\"v\">75 to 80%</td><td>Company-wide target; every site contributes</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<h2>GM sales responsibilities</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Review the previous day's conversion every morning and address gaps with the MSA team that day.</li>\n<li>Train every new MSA on W.A.S.H. before they work the lanes alone. Re-train anyone below standard.</li>\n<li>Coach MSAs live on the lot: observe pitches, give immediate feedback, and recognize wins.</li>\n<li>Actively promote MVP and Express Interiors to grow interior revenue.</li>\n<li>Work to retain members: resolve cancellation reasons, recover failed credit card payments, and protect the base.</li>\n</ul>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"What do the four letters in W.A.S.H. stand for?","o":["Wash, Ask, Sell, Hold","Welcome, Answer, Support, Hook","Welcome, Approach, Sell, Help","Wave, Answer, Smile, Hook"],"a":1,"why":"Welcome the vehicle, Answer questions, Support the fit, and Hook by asking for the sale."},{"q":"What is the standard conversion rate?","o":["10%","15 to 18%","25 to 35%","75 to 80%"],"a":1,"why":"15 to 18% is the standard, with 20%+ as the stretch goal in mature markets."},{"q":"A day with conversion below what level triggers an RM review?","o":["5%","10%","15%","18%"],"a":1,"why":"10% is the daily floor. Any day under it triggers an RM review and a conversation with leadership."},{"q":"What is an MVP member?","o":["A member for more than 12 months","Unlimited exterior plus Express Interiors","A fleet account","Any member on the Mighty Protector package"],"a":1,"why":"MVP = unlimited exterior + Express Interiors. The target is 25 to 35% of the member base."},{"q":"When should a new MSA be trained on W.A.S.H.?","o":["Within the first 30 days","Before they work the lanes alone","After their first sale","At the next GM meeting"],"a":1,"why":"Every new MSA is trained on W.A.S.H. before working the lanes alone, and anyone below standard is re-trained."},{"q":"What are the churn sub-targets inside the 7 to 9% monthly goal?","o":["Voluntary 3% or less, credit card 4% or less","Voluntary 5% or less, credit card 2% or less","Voluntary 7%, credit card 9%","There are no sub-targets"],"a":0,"why":"Voluntary churn 3% or less and credit card churn 4% or less. Recovering failed card payments is part of protecting the base."}]},{"key":"a4","title":"Labor and Scheduling","minutes":3,"why":"Labor is the largest cost the GM controls and the most direct lever on sales. Too many people and the budget breaks; too few at the wrong hour and members drive off unsold. Scheduling is a core competency because it is where the GM balances both, every week, by day and hour. A qualified leader on every shift matters because the standard is enforced by whoever is in charge, and if nobody is in charge the standard drops to whatever happens.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Mighty Wash General Managers are expected to be proficient in labor management and scheduling. This is a core competency of the role, not a support function.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Build schedules that match staffing to expected traffic by day and hour, including weekends and peak periods.</li>\n<li>Keep labor efficient and within budget without sacrificing customer experience or sales coverage.</li>\n<li>Always schedule enough MSAs to sell during peak volume. <strong>An unstaffed lane is a lost member.</strong></li>\n<li>Ensure a qualified leader is scheduled on every shift, including the GM's days off.</li>\n<li>Manage overtime, call-outs, and shift changes proactively. Adjust in real time when weather or volume changes.</li>\n<li>Publish schedules on time so team members can plan.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"eyebrow red\">Remember</div><p><em>An unstaffed lane is a lost member. Peak coverage is a sales decision, not just a labor decision.</em></p></div>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"Complete the standard: \"An unstaffed lane is a ...\"","o":["labor savings","lost member","maintenance risk","training opportunity"],"a":1,"why":"Enough MSAs to sell during peak volume is non-negotiable. An unstaffed lane is a lost member."},{"q":"What must be true of every shift, including the GM's days off?","o":["The GM is reachable by phone","A qualified leader is scheduled","At least four MSAs are on the lot","The RM has approved the schedule"],"a":1,"why":"A qualified leader is scheduled on every shift, including the GM's days off."},{"q":"How does Mighty Wash classify labor management and scheduling for GMs?","o":["A support function handled by the RM","A core competency of the role","An optional skill for larger sites","A corporate responsibility"],"a":1,"why":"It is a core competency of the role, not a support function."}]},{"key":"a5","title":"Administration","minutes":5,"why":"Each line protects something specific. Notes exist so a customer never has to re-explain their history to the next person who picks up the phone. The refund rule exists because quoting a number that corporate later calculates differently turns one complaint into two. A Hellowash ticket is a customer contact that already happened; an open one is a customer still waiting. Email, calendar, and cameras-on are how a multi-site company coordinates without being in the same room. Reviewing the sales reports catches cash and reporting errors while they are still small. Sunday and Monday coverage exists because customers do not know it is the GM's day off.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Strong administration protects the customer experience behind the scenes. These duties are part of the job and are reviewed alongside your operational results.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Area</th><th>Standard</th></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Fleet accounts</td><td>Contact and support Fleet accounts using the company's established process. Keep account information current.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Notes feature</td><td>Document every meaningful customer interaction, issue, and resolution in the Notes feature so anyone can pick up the history.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Refunds</td><td>Direct customers to contact corporate for refunds. Do not quote the refund amount to the customer.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Hellowash tickets</td><td>Review and close out Hellowash (AI answering service) tickets for your site promptly.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Email and calendar</td><td>Respond to emails in a timely manner and accept or decline every Google Calendar invite.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Virtual meetings</td><td>Cameras on for every Google Meet call.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Sales reporting</td><td>Review site sales reports (SiteWatch General Sales Report and Flexwash accounting report) and flag discrepancies.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Sundays and Mondays</td><td>Prioritize coverage and follow-up on Sundays and Mondays, the days GMs are typically off. Your site's standards do not take a day off.</td></tr>\n</table></div>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"A customer asks for a refund and wants to know how much they will get back. What do you do?","o":["Quote the amount and process it on site","Direct them to contact corporate and do not quote an amount","Give them a free wash instead","Ask the RM to approve an amount"],"a":1,"why":"Refunds go through corporate. Do not quote the refund amount to the customer."},{"q":"What is Hellowash?","o":["The nightly cleaning checklist","The AI answering service whose tickets you close out promptly","The fleet account portal","The scheduling tool"],"a":1,"why":"Hellowash is the AI answering service. Review and close its tickets for your site promptly."},{"q":"Which two reports does a GM review for discrepancies?","o":["Google Calendar and Notes","SiteWatch General Sales Report and Flexwash accounting report","Hellowash and the fleet log","The RM camera log and labor report"],"a":1,"why":"Review the SiteWatch General Sales Report and the Flexwash accounting report and flag discrepancies."},{"q":"Why do Sundays and Mondays get special attention?","o":["They are the busiest days","They are the days GMs are typically off, and standards do not take a day off","Corporate audits happen then","Fleet accounts are billed then"],"a":1,"why":"GMs are typically off Sundays and Mondays. Prioritize coverage and follow-up so the site holds its standards."},{"q":"What is the expectation for Google Meet calls?","o":["Join by phone if busy","Cameras on for every call","Only the RM needs a camera","Optional during peak hours"],"a":1,"why":"Cameras on for every Google Meet call."}]},{"key":"a6","title":"Bonuses and Career Growth","minutes":3,"why":"The bonus drivers line up with the standards on purpose. If the bonus paid on one number, GMs would chase that number at the expense of everything else. Tying it to sales, retention, customer experience, labor, and administration means the GM who runs a complete site is the one who earns the most. Good standing as the eligibility rule means the standards are the job, not a side list. The Road Map to Success exists so the path to more pay and more responsibility is written down and the same for everyone, instead of depending on who you know.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Mighty Wash takes care of its people. The Road Map to Success shows every team member what they can earn, in pay and bonuses, as they grow. GM bonuses reward the results these standards are built around.</p>\n<h2>What drives your bonus</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sales and conversion:</strong> hitting conversion, member growth, and MVP penetration targets.</li>\n<li><strong>Retention:</strong> keeping churn within target and protecting the member base.</li>\n<li><strong>Customer experience:</strong> site readiness, cleanliness, audit results, and customer feedback.</li>\n<li><strong>Labor:</strong> running efficient, well-covered schedules within budget.</li>\n<li><strong>Administration and professionalism:</strong> consistent follow-through on the standards in this program.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Bonus amounts and payout timing follow the current Mighty Wash GM bonus plan. <strong>Bonus eligibility requires the GM to be in good standing on these standards.</strong></p>\n<h2>Growing with Mighty Wash</h2>\n<p>GMs who consistently meet and exceed these standards are the leaders Mighty Wash develops and promotes. Ask your Regional Manager where you stand on the Road Map to Success and what it takes to reach the next level.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"What is required for bonus eligibility?","o":["Two years of tenure","Being in good standing on the GM standards","A conversion rate above 20%","RM nomination"],"a":1,"why":"Bonus eligibility requires the GM to be in good standing on the standards."},{"q":"Which of these is NOT one of the five bonus drivers?","o":["Retention","Labor","Number of social media posts","Customer experience"],"a":2,"why":"The five drivers are sales and conversion, retention, customer experience, labor, and administration and professionalism."},{"q":"What does the Road Map to Success show?","o":["Site maintenance priorities","What every team member can earn in pay and bonuses as they grow","The weekly schedule template","Fleet account targets"],"a":1,"why":"It shows what every team member can earn, in pay and bonuses, as they grow. Ask your RM where you stand on it."}]},{"key":"a7","title":"How Performance Is Reviewed","minutes":3,"why":"A fixed review rhythm removes guesswork about where you stand. Daily checks cover the things that have to happen every single day. Weekly site visits handle maintenance and coaching while problems are still cheap. Quarterly reviews look forward instead of back. Coaching comes first because most misses are fixable once they are named, and a documented improvement plan comes second so repeated gaps are handled fairly and the same way at every site.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Performance is reviewed on a fixed rhythm, and most of it is observable: cameras, reports, site visits, and quarterly planning.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Frequency</th><th>What is reviewed</th><th>By</th></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Daily</td><td>On-time opening, test car, nightly clean, previous-day conversion</td><td>Regional Manager (camera and reports)</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Weekly</td><td>Site visits, maintenance priorities, coaching plan for GMs needing support</td><td>Regional Manager</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Ongoing</td><td>Site audits, MSA sales training, customer experience coaching</td><td>Regional Manager</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Quarterly</td><td>Performance review and forward planning at the GM Quarterly Meeting</td><td>VP of Customer Experience and Field Leadership</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"eyebrow red\">When a standard is missed</div><p>Falling short of a standard leads to coaching first. Repeated or serious gaps are addressed through a documented improvement plan.</p></div>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"Who leads the quarterly performance review?","o":["The Regional Manager","The VP of Customer Experience and Field Leadership, at the GM Quarterly Meeting","Corporate HR","The site GM, as a self-review"],"a":1,"why":"Quarterly review and forward planning happen at the GM Quarterly Meeting, led by the VP of Customer Experience and Field Leadership."},{"q":"What does the daily review cover?","o":["Labor budget and overtime","On-time opening, test car, nightly clean, and previous-day conversion","Fleet account status","Hellowash tickets"],"a":1,"why":"Daily: on-time opening, test car, nightly clean, and previous-day conversion, verified by the RM through cameras and reports."},{"q":"What is the first response when a GM falls short of a standard?","o":["A written warning","Coaching","Loss of bonus eligibility","A documented improvement plan"],"a":1,"why":"Coaching comes first. Repeated or serious gaps move to a documented improvement plan."}]}]},{"title":"Setting and Holding the Standard","blurb":"How expectations are set, why they slip, and how to hold the line, from hiring day forward.","note":"Mighty Wash Leadership Development Series","draft":false,"lessons":[{"key":"b1","title":"The Core Principle","minutes":4,"why":"Everything else in this course rests on this idea. If people learned the standard from the handbook, training would be enough and this course would not exist. They learn it from what gets enforced, which means the GM's daily behavior is the real training program at the site. Understanding that most drift is people matching what looks normal, not defiance, changes how you respond: with a clear correction and a visible standard instead of a lecture about attitude.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">People do not calibrate their effort and behavior to what is written in a handbook or said in an interview. They calibrate to what actually gets enforced: what they watch happen, day after day, on the lot and in the break room.</p>\n<p>This is sometimes summed up as: <strong>\"The standard you walk past is the standard you accept.\"</strong> Whatever a GM allows to slide becomes the real standard for that site, no matter what the handbook says.</p>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"eyebrow red\">Core idea</div><p><em>New hires do not learn the rules from what you tell them at hiring. They learn the rules from what they see enforced in their first two weeks.</em></p></div>\n<h2>It is usually not about getting away with something</h2>\n<p>It is tempting to describe this as employees always doing what they can get away with, as if people are looking for an angle. That is mostly not what is happening. Most people are not testing limits out of some instinct to shirk. They are reading the room and matching whatever behavior looks normal on the team.</p>\n<p>A new MSA who sees a veteran skip greeting customers on the lot does not think \"I am getting away with something.\" They think \"I guess that is not actually required here.\"</p>\n<p>The practical result is the same either way: <strong>inconsistent enforcement teaches people the real rules faster than any onboarding conversation does.</strong></p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"According to the manual, what do people calibrate their behavior to?","o":["What the handbook says","What was said in the interview","What actually gets enforced, day after day","What the bonus plan rewards"],"a":2,"why":"People calibrate to what they watch get enforced on the lot and in the break room, not to the handbook."},{"q":"Complete the line: \"The standard you walk past is the standard you ...\"","o":["forget","accept","coach","report"],"a":1,"why":"Whatever a GM allows to slide becomes the real standard for that site."},{"q":"How does the manual describe most employees who drift from a standard?","o":["They are testing limits on purpose","They are reading the room and matching what looks normal","They did not read the handbook","They are waiting for a bonus incentive"],"a":1,"why":"Most people are not shirking. They match whatever looks normal on the team, which is why consistent enforcement matters so much."},{"q":"When do new hires learn the real rules?","o":["At the interview","During the handbook review","From what they see enforced in their first two weeks","At their 90-day review"],"a":2,"why":"New hires learn the rules from what they see enforced in their first two weeks."}]},{"key":"b2","title":"Why Expectations Erode","minutes":3,"why":"You cannot hold a standard you cannot see slipping. The four erosion points are the exact moments where it happens, and each one feels reasonable at the time: the veteran has earned some slack, the new hire will figure it out, it was not worth the confrontation today. Naming them lets you recognize the moment while it is still one exception, before it becomes the new normal that every new hire learns.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Expectations do not usually collapse all at once. They erode in small, quiet moments, almost always because a GM is trying to avoid friction.</p>\n<h2>The four most common erosion points</h2>\n<ul class=\"steps\">\n<li>A veteran employee breaks a known rule, and nothing visibly happens.</li>\n<li>A new hire is held to a different (lower) standard than what was described at hiring, and no one corrects it.</li>\n<li>Diligence and corner-cutting get the same outcome: same schedule, same recognition, same bonus.</li>\n<li>A GM enforces a rule only when a customer complains, not as a baseline.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Each of these sends the same message: <strong>the real standard is lower than the stated one.</strong> And once that message lands with one employee, it spreads, because new hires watch tenured staff to figure out what is actually required, not what is in the handbook.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"Which of these is one of the four common erosion points?","o":["A new hire asks too many questions","A veteran breaks a known rule and nothing visibly happens","The schedule is published late","A customer leaves a bad review"],"a":1,"why":"All four erosion points involve a visible gap between the stated standard and what actually happens."},{"q":"Why do expectations usually erode?","o":["Because the handbook is unclear","Because a GM is trying to avoid friction in small, quiet moments","Because corporate changes the rules","Because new hires are poorly screened"],"a":1,"why":"Erosion happens in small, quiet moments, almost always because a GM is avoiding friction."},{"q":"What message does enforcing a rule only after a customer complains send to the team?","o":["Customers are the priority","The real standard is lower than the stated one","The GM is strict","Complaints are rare"],"a":1,"why":"Every erosion point sends the same message: the real standard is lower than the stated one. Then it spreads."}]},{"key":"b3","title":"The Hiring Window","minutes":4,"why":"A new hire arrives with no model of what normal looks like at your site and builds one within days. That makes the first two weeks the cheapest place to set a standard and the most expensive place to lose one. Specific, observable standards matter because \"customer-focused\" cannot be corrected, but \"greet within the first 10 feet\" can. The pairing rule exists because whoever the new hire stands next to in those first shifts does more teaching than you will.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">The hiring window is your highest-leverage moment. Whatever you say in the interview and the first week sets a new hire's baseline expectation. But that baseline only survives if it matches what they observe on the floor. If it does not, the observed standard wins, every time.</p>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"eyebrow red\">Rule of thumb</div><p><em>A new hire will hold your stated standard for about as long as it takes them to see one exception go unaddressed.</em></p></div>\n<h2>At hiring, be specific, not aspirational</h2>\n<p>\"We are customer-focused\" is aspirational and unenforceable. \"Every associate greets customers within the first 10 feet of the lot, every shift\" is specific and observable. Specific standards are easier to hold because everyone, including you, can tell instantly whether they are being met.</p>\n<h2>Show, do not just tell</h2>\n<p>Where possible, pair the new hire with your most consistent performer in their first shifts, not just whoever is available. Their first mental model of \"normal\" gets built from whoever they are standing next to.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"Which of these is a specific, observable standard?","o":["\"We are customer-focused\"","\"Every associate greets customers within the first 10 feet of the lot, every shift\"","\"Treat people the way you want to be treated\"","\"Give it your best every day\""],"a":1,"why":"A specific standard names a behavior anyone can see being met or missed. A value statement cannot be enforced."},{"q":"Who should a new hire be paired with in their first shifts?","o":["Whoever is available","Your most consistent performer","The newest MSA, so they learn together","The RM"],"a":1,"why":"Their first model of normal is built from whoever they stand next to. Make that your most consistent performer."},{"q":"How long will a new hire hold your stated standard?","o":["Through their 90-day review","About as long as it takes to see one exception go unaddressed","Until the next team meeting","Indefinitely, if it was said at hiring"],"a":1,"why":"The stated standard survives only until the new hire sees one exception go unaddressed."}]},{"key":"b4","title":"Holding the Line After Hiring","minutes":5,"why":"Setting a standard costs nothing. Holding one costs a small amount of friction, repeatedly, often with people you like and depend on. The four habits keep that cost manageable. Even enforcement removes the sense of favoritism that kills a standard faster than anything else. Correcting in the moment is what makes the correction read as the rule. Saying the standard out loud teaches everyone within earshot, not just the person being corrected. Recognition makes meeting the standard the thing that gets noticed, so the team is never left guessing whether consistency actually matters here.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Setting the expectation is the easy part. Holding it, especially with a tenured employee, or when you are short-staffed and do not want to make waves, is where most sites actually lose the standard.</p>\n<h2>Four habits that protect the standard</h2>\n<ul class=\"steps\">\n<li><strong>Enforce evenly.</strong> The newest hire and your longest-tenured MSA are held to the same rule, in view of each other, without exception.</li>\n<li><strong>Correct in the moment, not later.</strong> A correction given a week after the fact reads as a suggestion. A correction given on the spot reads as the actual standard.</li>\n<li><strong>Say the standard out loud, not just the correction.</strong> \"We greet every customer, every time. Let's go say hi to that car\" teaches the rule. \"Hey, come here a sec\" does not.</li>\n<li><strong>Recognize the standard being met, not just violations of it.</strong> If the employee who does it right never hears about it, and the one who skips it only hears about it when caught, you have quietly told the team that consistency is not valued, just avoiding getting caught.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"eyebrow red\">Watch for</div><p><em>If you find yourself thinking \"I'll let it go this once,\" that is usually the moment the real standard is being reset. Not the exception, the new normal.</em></p></div>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"How does a correction given a week after the fact read to the team?","o":["As the actual standard","As a suggestion","As a formal warning","As coaching"],"a":1,"why":"A correction on the spot reads as the standard. A week later, it reads as a suggestion."},{"q":"Which phrasing teaches the rule?","o":["\"Hey, come here a sec\"","\"We greet every customer, every time. Let's go say hi to that car\"","\"You know better than that\"","\"Do not let me see that again\""],"a":1,"why":"Say the standard out loud, not just the correction, so the rule itself is what the team hears."},{"q":"If you only ever correct misses and never recognize the standard being met, what does the team learn?","o":["The GM is fair","Consistency is not valued, just avoiding getting caught","Standards are flexible","Recognition comes later"],"a":1,"why":"Recognition is how the team learns that meeting the standard is what is actually valued."},{"q":"What does \"I'll let it go this once\" usually signal?","o":["Reasonable flexibility","The moment the real standard is being reset to a new normal","Good judgment under pressure","A one-time exception with no lasting effect"],"a":1,"why":"That thought is usually the moment the real standard resets. It becomes the new normal, not the exception."}]},{"key":"b5","title":"Set, Show, Enforce, Recognize","minutes":3,"why":"The framework exists so you can diagnose where a standard broke instead of guessing. If the team does not know the standard, the gap is Set. If they learned the wrong version from a veteran, it is Show. If they know it and skip it, it is Enforce. If the people who do it right are starting to drift, it is Recognize. Each step closes one specific gap, which is why skipping any one of them undoes the other three.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">A simple framework ties the whole manual together. Each step covers a gap the others cannot.</p>\n<div class=\"loop\">\n<div><div class=\"st\">SET</div><p>Define the standard in specific, observable terms at hiring. Not a value statement, a behavior.</p></div>\n<div><div class=\"st\">SHOW</div><p>Pair new hires with your most consistent performer so their model of normal is accurate from day one.</p></div>\n<div><div class=\"st\">ENFORCE</div><p>Correct every miss, from every employee, in the moment. Not just when it is convenient or a customer is watching.</p></div>\n<div><div class=\"st\">RECOGNIZE</div><p>Make sure the people meeting the standard visibly get something for it: attention, praise, or advancement.</p></div>\n</div>\n<h2>Skipping any one step breaks the loop</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Set without Enforce</strong> is just a poster on the wall.</li>\n<li><strong>Enforce without Recognize</strong> makes the standard feel punitive instead of normal.</li>\n<li><strong>Show without consistent role models</strong> undoes the other three.</li>\n</ul>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"What are the four steps of the framework, in order?","o":["Set, Show, Enforce, Recognize","Hire, Train, Correct, Reward","Welcome, Answer, Support, Hook","Set, Enforce, Review, Promote"],"a":0,"why":"Set the standard, Show it through role models, Enforce every miss in the moment, Recognize the standard being met."},{"q":"\"Set without Enforce\" is ...","o":["a strong start","just a poster on the wall","enough for tenured teams","the RM's job"],"a":1,"why":"A standard that is defined but never enforced is just a poster on the wall."},{"q":"What happens with Enforce but no Recognize?","o":["The team works harder","The standard feels punitive instead of normal","Turnover drops","Nothing changes"],"a":1,"why":"Without recognition, enforcement makes the standard feel punitive instead of normal."},{"q":"What does SHOW mean in practice?","o":["Post the standards in the break room","Pair new hires with your most consistent performer","Demonstrate the W.A.S.H. pitch once","Share the camera footage"],"a":1,"why":"Show means pairing new hires with your most consistent performer so their model of normal is accurate from day one."}]},{"key":"b6","title":"GM Self-Check","minutes":5,"why":"Standards drift quietly, and the GM is usually the last to notice because the drift happens around them, one reasonable exception at a time. This check turns the whole manual into six observable questions about your own site this week. It ends with one focus item because fixing one thing this week works, and trying to fix six at once does not.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Use this before your next site walk or team meeting. Be honest. This is a diagnostic, not a report card.</p>\n<p>Check each statement that is true at your site right now. If you cannot check every box, that is the starting point, not a failure. Pick the one item you cannot check and make it the thing you fix this week.</p>","kind":"selfcheck","checks":["Every new hire on my site heard the same specific standard I hold veterans to.","I corrected the last standard-miss I saw in the moment, not after the fact.","The last correction I gave was given the same way regardless of who it was, new hire or veteran.","I have recognized someone this week specifically for meeting the standard, not just for hitting a number.","There is no rule on my site right now that I am quietly not enforcing.","If a new hire only learned our standards by watching (not by being told), they would learn the right ones."],"quiz":[]}]},{"title":"Level 3 Maintenance","blurb":null,"note":"Draft course, not part of the issued GM Standards","draft":true,"lessons":[{"key":"a8","title":"Owning the Equipment and the R&M Budget","minutes":7,"why":"The GM Standards say the site is yours: its performance, appearance, and reputation. Equipment is where appearance and performance meet the budget. A GM who runs the tunnel to failure spends more, loses more Saturdays, and washes worse cars than one who runs a maintenance program and plans replacements. Level 3 is the management layer above the wrench: budget, uptime, lifecycle, and the people who hold the qualifications below you.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">You do not need to turn the wrench. You need to make sure the program runs, the money is spent on the right things, and the tunnel is up when the cars are there.</p>\n<h2>Four numbers you own</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>Number</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Downtime hours per month</td><td>Every hour the tunnel is down at peak is lost cars and lost Members. Track cause, not just hours.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">R&M spend vs budget</td><td>Repair and maintenance has a budget. Know it monthly. Over because of one failure is explainable; over because of no PM is not.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Cost per car</td><td>Chemical, water, and electricity per car washed. A rising number usually means a leak, a miscalibrated tip, or a reclaim problem.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">PM completion</td><td>Percent of scheduled PM tasks logged on time. Under 90% is the leading indicator of next month's downtime.</td></tr></table></div>\n<h2>Lifecycle and capital</h2>\n<ul><li>Every major component has a life: cloth, brushes, dryer motors, conveyor chain, pumps, pay stations. Know roughly where each one is in that life.</li><li>When a component is patched three times, it goes on the replacement list with a cost, a lead time, and the downtime it will cause if it fails first.</li><li>Capital requests go to leadership with the cost, the failure risk, the customer impact, and the payback. \"The dryer is old\" is not a request; \"the dryer has failed twice this quarter, a third failure on a Saturday costs about X cars, and replacement is Y\" is.</li></ul>\n<h2>The qualifications on your team</h2>\n<ul><li>You certify Level 1 and Level 2 on your site. Watch them do it; do not sign off on a quiz score alone.</li><li>Every shift has at least one Level 1 on it. The AGM holds Level 2.</li><li>Audit lockout the way the RM audits your test car: unannounced, in the moment, with the standard said out loud.</li></ul>\n<h2>Vendors and contracts</h2>\n<p>Know what the service agreement covers, the response time it promises, and what it costs when they miss. Hold them to it the way the RM holds you to the standards.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"PM completion fell to 70% last month. What does that predict?","o":["Nothing; the tunnel is running","Next month's downtime","Lower chemical cost","A better audit"],"a":1,"why":"PM completion is the leading indicator. Downtime follows missed PM by weeks, not days."},{"q":"Which is a capital request leadership can decide on?","o":["\"The dryer is old\"","\"The dryer failed twice this quarter; a Saturday failure costs about 200 cars; replacement is $X with a 6-week lead time\"","\"We need a new dryer\"","\"The vendor recommends replacing it\""],"a":1,"why":"Cost, failure risk, customer impact, and payback. That is a decision; the rest are opinions."},{"q":"How do you certify a Level 1 on your site?","o":["Quiz score of 100%","Watch them do the task to standard, including lockout, then sign off","The AGM says they are ready","Tenure of 90 days"],"a":1,"why":"Watch the work. A quiz proves knowledge; the sign-off certifies the behavior."},{"q":"Cost per car has risen 15% with no price change in chemical. Most likely?","o":["More cars","A leak, a miscalibrated tip, or a reclaim problem","Weather","The vendor"],"a":1,"why":"Rising cost per car is almost always a physical problem on the site. Walk it with the Captain."}]},{"key":"a9","title":"Technology, Data, and Security Ownership","minutes":7,"why":"A GM runs the site on reports and systems: FlexWash, SiteWatch, the cameras, the plate reader, Hellowash, Google Workspace. If the data in them is wrong, every daily number is wrong, and the GM is managing a site that does not exist. If access to them is loose, one departed employee or one bad click can cost more than a quarter's downtime. Level 3 is making sure the systems tell the truth and only the right people can touch them.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Own the systems the site runs on: who has access, whether the data is right, and what happens when something goes wrong.</p>\n<h2>Access control</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Quarterly access review:</strong> every user on every system, matched to the current roster. Anyone not on the roster is removed that day. Sign the review and keep it.</li><li>Permissions match roles. An MSA does not have price-change rights. A Supervisor does not have admin rights. You do not share yours.</li><li>Offboarding is same day, every system, checklist signed. Ask the AGM for the checklist, not the assurance.</li></ul>\n<h2>Data integrity</h2>\n<ul><li>The SiteWatch General Sales Report and the Flexwash accounting report are reconciled daily by the AGM and reviewed by you weekly. Discrepancies are investigated and the cause written down, not adjusted away.</li><li>Membership data hygiene: duplicate accounts merged, plates correct, failed payments worked weekly from the report. Churn numbers are only as good as the account data.</li><li>Price and package changes follow the company process and are verified at the pay station after the change. A price wrong at the lane is a refund problem by noon.</li></ul>\n<h2>Security and incident response</h2>\n<ul><li>Nobody at the site writes down or stores card numbers. Card data lives in the terminal and the processor. Period.</li><li>A suspected incident (ransomware, locked files, unknown accounts, a device you do not recognize, a phishing click) is reported to leadership and IT within the hour. The site does not try to fix it and never pays.</li><li>Phishing and social engineering: no one legitimate asks for a password, a code, or gift cards. Train it, test it, and recognize the person who reports it.</li><li>Site devices are site devices: no personal accounts, no downloads, screens locked.</li></ul>\n<h2>Rollouts and vendors</h2>\n<ul><li>A new system or a POS change is a project: a date, a test lane, a trained team before go-live, and a rollback plan. The first Saturday after a change gets the GM on the lot.</li><li>Hellowash and any AI-assisted tool is reviewed, not just closed. Read a sample of tickets weekly: is the answer the customer got the answer you would have given?</li><li>Vendor escalations go with a ticket number, a time line, and the business impact. \"It is still down\" gets a slower answer than \"Lane 2 has been down 3 hours on a Saturday, ticket 4471.\"</li></ul>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"What is the quarterly access review?","o":["Checking who has keys","Every user on every system matched to the current roster, with extras removed that day and the review signed","Changing all passwords","A vendor audit"],"a":1,"why":"Every system, every user, against the roster, every quarter, signed."},{"q":"The two sales reports disagree by $140 on Tuesday. The AGM proposes adjusting the entry so they match. You ...","o":["approve it; it is small","investigate the cause and write it down; discrepancies are explained, not adjusted away","ignore it under $200","report it to the RM only"],"a":1,"why":"A discrepancy adjusted away is a problem hidden. Find the cause and document it."},{"q":"A Supervisor clicked a link in an email and entered their POS password before realizing. You ...","o":["tell them to change it tomorrow","report to leadership and IT within the hour, have the password changed now, and treat it as an incident","write them up","do nothing if nothing looks wrong"],"a":1,"why":"A phished credential is an incident. Within the hour, report and reset. Then train it and recognize the report."},{"q":"How should Hellowash be managed?","o":["Close tickets promptly and nothing else","Close tickets promptly and review a weekly sample: did the customer get the answer you would have given?","Turn it off on weekends","Let corporate handle it"],"a":1,"why":"An AI tool is reviewed, not just closed. A weekly sample tells you whether it is representing the site well."}]},{"key":"a10","title":"Facilities, Compliance, and Risk","minutes":7,"why":"The GM Standards put site readiness, cleanliness, audit results, and customer feedback in the bonus. Facilities are most of what those words mean. Compliance and risk are the part the standards do not spell out because they assume it: a GM who lets an inspection lapse, an injury go unreported, or a contractor work uninsured has put the site, the company, and people at risk. Level 3 is the annual plan, the compliance calendar, and the judgment calls.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Run the site like you own the building: a plan for the year, a calendar that never surprises you, and a clear head when something goes wrong.</p>\n<h2>The annual facilities plan</h2>\n<ul><li>Once a year, walk the whole site with the AGM and the RM. List everything that will need attention in the next 12 months: pavement, lighting, signage, canopy, vacuums, restrooms, landscaping, paint.</li><li>Sort it: repair this quarter, repair this year, capital request. Put dates and rough costs on it.</li><li>Curb appeal is marketing. A faded sign and a cracked lot say the wash is tired before the customer has seen a car come out. Budget for the entrance the way you budget for the tunnel.</li></ul>\n<h2>The compliance calendar</h2>\n<p>Every item below has an owner (usually the AGM), a due date, and a 30-day warning. You review it monthly and bring it to the RM quarterly.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>Item</th><th>Cadence</th></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Fire extinguishers and life safety</td><td>Monthly tag check; annual vendor inspection</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Backflow preventer test</td><td>Annual, certified tester, filed with the utility</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Water discharge and reclaim</td><td>Per permit; never chemical to the storm drain; spill kit stocked</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Chemical storage and SDS</td><td>SDS binder current; containment; eyewash tested monthly</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Injury and incident log</td><td>Every injury same shift; serious injuries escalated immediately; records kept per policy</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Permits and licenses</td><td>Business, sign, health where applicable; renewal dates on the calendar</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Contractor insurance</td><td>Certificate on file before any contractor works on site</td></tr></table></div>\n<h2>Risk and judgment</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Injuries:</strong> care for the person first. Then document, report, and preserve footage. Never discourage a report.</li><li><strong>Damage claims:</strong> reviewed by you within 24 hours with the Captain's documentation and footage, then sent to corporate for decision. You promise a timeline, not an outcome.</li><li><strong>Severe weather, power outage, chemical spill:</strong> the site has a written plan for each, the team knows where it is, and you have walked through it once this year.</li><li><strong>Contractors:</strong> insured, checked in, nowhere near energized equipment. A contractor injury on your site is your incident.</li><li><strong>Security:</strong> lighting works at night, cameras cover the lanes and the booth, and any cash on site is handled by two people and dropped daily.</li></ul>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"What goes on the annual facilities plan?","o":["Only capital items","Everything that will need attention in 12 months, sorted into this quarter, this year, and capital, with dates and rough costs","The vendor list","The nightly clean checklist"],"a":1,"why":"One list, sorted and dated, built on a walk with the AGM and RM."},{"q":"A team member is hurt and says it is nothing. You ...","o":["take their word for it","care for them first, then document, report the same shift, and preserve footage; never discourage a report","note it if they are still hurting tomorrow","ask them not to report it to keep the record clean"],"a":1,"why":"Every injury, same shift, every time. Discouraging a report is the one thing a GM must never do."},{"q":"Why is curb appeal in the facilities budget?","o":["The RM likes it","A faded sign and a cracked lot say the wash is tired before the customer sees a car come out; the entrance is marketing","It is not; marketing pays for it","For the audit score only"],"a":1,"why":"The entrance is the first impression and the cheapest marketing the site has."},{"q":"A contractor shows up without an insurance certificate on file. You ...","o":["let them work; they came a long way","do not let them start until the certificate is on file","let them work outside the tunnel only","ask the AGM to decide"],"a":1,"why":"Certificate on file before any contractor works on site. A contractor injury on your site is your incident."}]}]}],"scenarios":[{"t":"The veteran at the vacuums","s":"Your longest-tenured MSA, also your best seller, walks past two customers at the vacuums without greeting them. Your newest hire, three days in, is standing next to him and sees it.","o":["Say nothing. He is your best seller and the new hire will figure it out.","Correct it right there, say the standard out loud, and bring both of them over to greet the customers.","Make a note and bring it up at next week's team meeting.","Wait to see if a customer complains before acting."],"a":1,"why":"Enforce evenly and in the moment, and say the standard out loud. The new hire is building their model of normal from exactly this moment."},{"t":"Short an MSA on Saturday","s":"Saturday is forecast to be heavy and an MSA just called out. You have enough people to run the tunnel but the second pay lane will have no one selling.","o":["Run with who you have. Labor stays under budget.","Adjust the schedule in real time to put an MSA on that lane, pulling from elsewhere or calling someone in.","Close the second lane for the day.","Email the RM and wait for direction."],"a":1,"why":"Adjust in real time when volume changes. An unstaffed lane is a lost member, and peak coverage is a sales decision."},{"t":"The refund at the pay station","s":"A member at the pay station says she was double-charged last month and asks how much she will get back.","o":["Look up the charge and tell her the exact amount so she feels taken care of.","Apologize, direct her to contact corporate for the refund, document it in Notes, and do not quote an amount.","Refund it from the register.","Offer a free Express Interior instead of a refund."],"a":1,"why":"Refunds go through corporate and the amount is never quoted on site. Documenting the interaction in Notes lets anyone pick up the history."},{"t":"Yesterday was 8%","s":"You open the report and yesterday's conversion came in at 8%.","o":["Wait for the RM to call about it.","Review it with the MSA team that morning, coach live on the lot today, and expect an RM review since the day was under the 10% floor.","Note it and watch the weekly average.","Adjust the site target so the day looks closer to standard."],"a":1,"why":"Previous-day conversion is reviewed every morning and gaps are addressed that day. Any day under 10% triggers an RM review."},{"t":"The late open","s":"The opener was late and the site opened 15 minutes after posted hours. No customers were waiting and no one has said anything.","o":["Let it go. Nobody noticed.","Report it to leadership immediately, then address the miss with the opener today.","Mention it to the RM at the weekly site visit.","Record the open as on time since no customer was affected."],"a":1,"why":"Late openings are reported to leadership immediately, and the RM verifies opening time by camera anyway. Correct the miss in the moment."},{"t":"First shifts for a new hire","s":"A new MSA starts tomorrow. Your most consistent performer is scheduled, but so is a tenured MSA who tends to cut corners and happens to be free.","o":["Pair the new hire with whoever has the lightest workload.","Pair the new hire with your most consistent performer, even if it takes some rearranging.","Let the new hire rotate between everyone.","Have the new hire shadow you all day instead."],"a":1,"why":"Show, do not just tell. The new hire's first mental model of normal comes from whoever they stand next to."},{"t":"The third patch","s":"The AGM reports the main dryer motor has been repaired three times this quarter. The vendor can patch it again for $900 or replace it for $9,000 with a six-week lead time.","o":["Patch it; $900 is in budget.","Patch it to stay running, and submit a capital request today with the failure history, the Saturday downtime risk in cars, the replacement cost, and the lead time.","Replace it without asking.","Run without a dryer if it fails."],"a":1,"why":"Keep it running, and turn the pattern into a decision leadership can make: cost, risk, customer impact, payback."},{"t":"The phished password","s":"A Supervisor tells you she entered her POS password on a link in an email that looked like it came from the POS vendor. Nothing looks wrong yet.","o":["Tell her to change it when she gets a chance.","Report to leadership and IT within the hour, reset the password now, treat it as an incident, and thank her for reporting it in front of the team.","Write her up.","Wait to see if anything happens."],"a":1,"why":"A phished credential is an incident regardless of what has happened yet. Report, reset, and recognize the report so the next person speaks up too."}],"assessment":[{"q":"Which best describes what \"ownership\" means for a Mighty Wash GM?","o":["Running the site during scheduled shifts","Treating the site like your own business, seven days a week","Reporting results to the RM weekly","Managing the MSA team only"],"a":1},{"q":"What three things does the Regional Manager verify by camera each morning?","o":["Sales Booth staffing, dress code, cell phone use","Opening time, the test car, and the nightly clean","Conversion, churn, and labor","Fleet calls, Notes, and Hellowash tickets"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the H in W.A.S.H. require?","o":["Hand the customer a brochure","Ask every eligible customer for the sale, every time","Hold the lane until the customer decides","Highlight Mighty Protector"],"a":1},{"q":"Membership should make up what share of company revenue?","o":["25 to 35%","50%","75 to 80%","90%+"],"a":2},{"q":"What is the MVP penetration target?","o":["10%","15 to 18%","25 to 35% of the member base","75 to 80% of the member base"],"a":2},{"q":"A GM's day off is Sunday. Who covers the site standard that day?","o":["Nobody; standards resume Tuesday","A qualified leader scheduled on the shift, with the GM prioritizing coverage and follow-up","The RM","Corporate"],"a":1},{"q":"Where should every meaningful customer interaction be documented?","o":["In an email to the RM","In the Notes feature","In the Hellowash ticket","In the SiteWatch report"],"a":1},{"q":"What is the first response when a GM misses a standard?","o":["Loss of bonus","A documented improvement plan","Coaching","A written warning"],"a":2},{"q":"According to the Leadership Manual, what teaches people the real rules fastest?","o":["The handbook","The interview","Inconsistent enforcement","The bonus plan"],"a":2},{"q":"Which of these is an erosion point?","o":["Diligence and corner-cutting get the same outcome","Publishing the schedule early","Recognizing a new hire in front of veterans","Running a test car every morning"],"a":0},{"q":"A correction given on the spot reads as ...","o":["a suggestion","the actual standard","micromanagement","a formal warning"],"a":1},{"q":"Which step of the framework makes sure people meeting the standard visibly get something for it?","o":["Set","Show","Enforce","Recognize"],"a":3},{"q":"The leading indicator of next month's downtime is ...","o":["car count","PM completion rate","chemical cost","the weather"],"a":1},{"q":"A quarterly access review matches every system user to ...","o":["the schedule","the current roster, with extras removed that day","the vendor list","payroll"],"a":1},{"q":"A report discrepancy is ...","o":["adjusted to match","investigated and documented","ignored under $200","the AGM's problem"],"a":1},{"q":"Every injury is reported ...","o":["if serious","the same shift, every time","weekly","at the quarterly meeting"],"a":1}]},{"key":"l7","num":7,"title":"Regional Manager","short":"Regional Manager","role":"You own a region of sites and get results through General Managers. You verify daily, visit weekly, coach continuously, and review quarterly with the VP.","blurb":"Multiplying GMs, the daily verification rhythm, site visits that change behavior, coaching and improvement plans, developing the bench, reading the region, and the quarterly meeting.","why":"The GM Standards name the Regional Manager as the person who verifies, visits, audits, and coaches. This level is the other side of that document: how an RM does those things so that seven or more sites run to one standard without the RM running any of them.","draft":true,"pass":80,"ack_formal":false,"ack_title":null,"ack_statement":"I have completed the Regional Manager training. I will verify every site daily, visit weekly with a structure and written commitments, coach first and document when needed, enforce the standards evenly across every GM, develop the bench, and bring a complete scorecard to the quarterly review.","courses":[{"title":"Regional Manager","blurb":null,"note":null,"draft":false,"lessons":[{"key":"r1","title":"Multiplying General Managers","minutes":4,"why":"A Regional Manager who runs the best site in the region has failed at the job. The RM's result is the region, and the region is only as good as its weakest GM on the RM's day off. Everything in this level is about getting results through people who own their own site.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">You do not run a site. You make GMs who run sites to the standard.</p>\n<h2>What the RM owns</h2>\n<ul><li>Every GM Standard, at every site, verified rather than assumed.</li><li>The daily check, the weekly visit, the ongoing audit and coaching, and the quarterly review, as laid out in the GM Standards.</li><li>GM hiring, development, and the documented improvement plan when coaching is not enough.</li><li>Consistency: the same standard enforced the same way at every site. The Leadership Manual applies to you with GMs exactly as it applies to GMs with MSAs.</li></ul>\n<h2>Results through GMs</h2>\n<ul><li>Ask, do not tell: \"What does your conversion say about yesterday, and what is your plan?\" The GM who brings the plan is the GM who owns the site.</li><li>Fix the system, not the symptom. One site under the floor is coaching; three sites under the floor is a regional problem you own.</li><li>Your time is the scarcest resource in the region. Spend it where the standard is slipping, not where it is comfortable.</li></ul>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"What is the RM's result?","o":["The best site in the region","The region: every site to the standard, including on the RM's day off","Personal site visits","The audit scores"],"a":1,"why":"The region is the result, and it is only as good as its weakest GM when the RM is not there."},{"q":"Three sites are under the conversion floor this week. What is that?","o":["Three coaching conversations","A regional problem the RM owns: find the common cause","Bad luck","The GMs' problem"],"a":1,"why":"One site is coaching. Three is a system problem: pricing, training, staffing, something regional."},{"q":"How does the RM apply the Leadership Manual?","o":["It is for GMs only","The same way GMs apply it to MSAs: specific standards, even enforcement, correction in the moment, recognition","Only at the quarterly meeting","By forwarding it"],"a":1,"why":"The standard you walk past is the standard you accept, at every level."}]},{"key":"r2","title":"The Daily Verification Rhythm","minutes":5,"why":"The GM Standards say the RM verifies opening time, the test car, and the nightly clean by camera every morning, and reviews previous-day conversion. This is in the standards because those four things fail quietly: a late open is not reported unless someone looks, and a site under the floor does not call to say so. Twenty minutes a day is how the RM knows instead of hopes.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Twenty minutes, every morning, every site, before anything else.</p>\n<h2>The four checks</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>Check</th><th>How</th><th>If it missed</th></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">On-time open</td><td>Camera timestamp vs posted hours</td><td>Call the GM today. Was it reported? If not, that is the bigger miss.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Test car</td><td>Camera: a vehicle through before the first customer</td><td>Call the GM. Who opened? Is the Captain trained?</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Nightly clean</td><td>Camera at close and at open: trash, vacuums, bay</td><td>Call the GM. Who closed? Was it verified against the checklist?</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Previous-day conversion</td><td>Report by site and MSA</td><td>Under 15%: ask for the plan. Under 10%: the RM review the standard requires, today.</td></tr></table></div>\n<h2>The conversation</h2>\n<p>Coaching first. \"Your site opened at 8:12. What happened, and what is the plan so it does not repeat?\" Then listen. Log the miss. A pattern over two or three weeks is what moves from coaching to a documented plan.</p>\n<h2>Make it a habit the GMs can feel</h2>\n<p>When GMs know the RM looks every morning, the standard holds on the RM's day off too. When they know the RM looks sometimes, it holds sometimes.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"What does the RM verify by camera every morning?","o":["Sales Booth staffing","On-time open, test car, and nightly clean","MSA pitches","Labor hours"],"a":1,"why":"Those three, plus previous-day conversion from the report."},{"q":"A site opened late and the GM did not report it. Which is the bigger miss?","o":["The late open","Not reporting it; late openings are reported to leadership immediately","Neither","The camera"],"a":1,"why":"Late opens happen. Hiding one breaks the standard that lets the RM manage."},{"q":"Conversion at a site was 8% yesterday. The standard requires ...","o":["a note for the weekly visit","an RM review today and a conversation with leadership","nothing until it repeats","a new MSA"],"a":1,"why":"Any day under 10% triggers an RM review and a conversation with leadership."},{"q":"What moves a GM from coaching to a documented improvement plan?","o":["A single miss","A repeated or serious gap after coaching","The quarterly meeting","The VP"],"a":1,"why":"Coaching first. Repeated or serious gaps are addressed through a documented improvement plan."}]},{"key":"r3","title":"Site Visits That Change Behavior","minutes":6,"why":"The GM Standards put site visits, maintenance priorities, and coaching plans on a weekly rhythm. A visit that is a tour changes nothing. A visit with a structure, observed behavior, and one to three commitments is how a GM's site gets better between visits. The structure below is so every GM in the region gets the same visit.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Every visit, same structure, so the GM knows what good looks like and you can compare sites.</p>\n<h2>Before you arrive</h2>\n<p>Last visit's commitments. This week's numbers. Open maintenance items. Any camera misses this week.</p>\n<h2>On site, in order</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li><strong>Arrive as a customer.</strong> Pull in, read the menu, use a pay lane, go through the wash, use the vacuums. Note everything a first-timer would.</li><li><strong>Watch the exit</strong> for ten cars. Quality, QC behavior, rewash offers.</li><li><strong>Watch an MSA</strong> work three cars. Full W.A.S.H.? The Hook?</li><li><strong>Walk the tunnel</strong> with the Captain. Startup log, chemical levels, maintenance list.</li><li><strong>Sit with the GM.</strong> Numbers: conversion, MVP, churn, labor. Last visit's commitments: done or not. Maintenance priorities. People: who is ready to move up, who needs coaching.</li><li><strong>Leave one to three commitments</strong>, written, with dates. Not ten.</li></ol>\n<h2>Coaching plan for GMs who need support</h2>\n<p>If a GM is behind on a standard, the visit ends with a coaching plan: the standard, the gap, what the GM will do, what you will do, when you will check. Written down and shared.</p>\n<h2>Audit</h2>\n<p>Site audits are ongoing. Score the same items the same way at every site so the scores mean something at the quarterly meeting.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"How does the site visit start?","o":["In the GM's office","As a customer: pay lane, wash, vacuums","With the audit form","With the tunnel walk"],"a":1,"why":"See the site the way a first-timer does before anyone knows you are there."},{"q":"How many commitments does a visit end with?","o":["As many as needed","One to three, written, with dates","Ten","None; the GM knows"],"a":1,"why":"One to three gets done. Ten gets filed."},{"q":"Why score audits the same way at every site?","o":["Corporate requires it","So the scores can be compared at the quarterly meeting and the standard is the same everywhere","It is faster","It is not important"],"a":1,"why":"Consistency is what makes an audit a management tool rather than an opinion."}]},{"key":"r4","title":"Coaching GMs and the Improvement Plan","minutes":5,"why":"The GM Standards promise coaching first and a documented improvement plan for repeated or serious gaps. That promise only holds if every RM coaches the same way and documents the same way. Uneven enforcement across GMs does to a region exactly what it does to a lot: the real standard becomes whatever the most lenient RM allows.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Coaching is a conversation with a structure. The improvement plan is that structure, written down.</p>\n<h2>The coaching conversation</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li><strong>The standard.</strong> \"The test car runs before the first customer, every day.\"</li><li><strong>The gap.</strong> \"Three of the last seven days, it did not.\"</li><li><strong>The cause,</strong> from the GM, not from you. \"What is getting in the way?\"</li><li><strong>The commitment.</strong> What the GM will do, by when.</li><li><strong>The check.</strong> When you will look, and what you will see if it worked.</li></ol>\n<h2>The documented improvement plan</h2>\n<p>Used when coaching has not closed a gap, or for a serious miss. It contains: the specific standards missed, with dates; the measure of success; the support you are providing; the timeline; the review dates; and what happens if the plan is not met. Signed by both. Shared with the VP.</p>\n<h2>Even enforcement</h2>\n<ul><li>Your highest-performing GM gets the same conversation about a late open as your newest.</li><li>Correct in the moment: the day of the miss, not at the quarterly meeting.</li><li>Say the standard, not just the correction.</li><li>Recognize the GM who holds the standard on a hard week, by name, to the region.</li></ul>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"In the coaching conversation, who names the cause?","o":["The RM","The GM, when asked what is getting in the way","The VP","The Supervisor"],"a":1,"why":"The GM owns the site. The GM names the cause and the commitment."},{"q":"What does a documented improvement plan contain?","o":["A warning","Specific standards missed with dates, the measure of success, support, timeline, review dates, and consequences","A list of complaints","A new bonus target"],"a":1,"why":"Specific, measurable, dated, supported, and signed."},{"q":"Your top GM opened late twice this week. What happens?","o":["Nothing; look at the numbers","The same coaching conversation your newest GM would get, this week","A note for the quarterly meeting","A text"],"a":1,"why":"Enforce evenly. The real standard in the region is whatever the most lenient treatment allows."}]},{"key":"r5","title":"Developing the Bench","minutes":4,"why":"The GM Standards tell GMs to ask their RM where they stand on the Road Map to Success. That only works if the RM has an answer. A region without a bench loses a site's standards every time a GM leaves, and loses good AGMs who did not see a path.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Every site should have a next GM in it. Your job is to know who, and to tell them.</p>\n<h2>Know the bench</h2>\n<ul><li>For every site: who is the AGM, are they running the site to standard on the GM's days off, and when will they be ready.</li><li>For every AGM: which Supervisor or Captain is next. Ask the GM at every visit.</li><li>Track Academy completion by level across the region. A Supervisor who has finished the AGM level is telling you something.</li></ul>\n<h2>The \"where you stand\" conversation</h2>\n<p>Once a quarter with every GM, and with every AGM through their GM: where they are on the Road Map, what the next level requires, what they need to show you, and by when. Specific and written. \"Keep it up\" is not an answer.</p>\n<h2>Promote the standard-holders</h2>\n<p>GMs who consistently meet and exceed the standards are the leaders the company develops and promotes. Promote on standards held, not on tenure or on who asks loudest. The region watches who gets promoted and learns what is valued.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"What should the RM know about every site's bench?","o":["Nothing; the GM handles it","Who the AGM is, whether they run the site to standard on GM days off, and who is next behind them","Only who is leaving","The payroll"],"a":1,"why":"A region without a bench loses a site's standard every time a GM leaves."},{"q":"How often is the \"where you stand\" conversation held?","o":["At hiring only","Once a quarter, specific and written","When someone asks","Annually"],"a":1,"why":"Quarterly, with the next level's requirements and a date."},{"q":"Who gets promoted?","o":["The longest tenured","Those who consistently meet and exceed the standards","Whoever asks","The highest conversion only"],"a":1,"why":"Promote on standards held. The region learns what is valued from who moves up."}]},{"key":"r6","title":"Reading the Region","minutes":5,"why":"A GM can act on one site's numbers. Only the RM can see that four sites dipped the same week, or that one site's churn is twice the others'. Reading the region is how the RM decides where to spend the week, and what is a site problem versus a company problem to bring to the VP.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Same five numbers, every site, side by side, every week.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>Number</th><th>Standard</th><th>What an outlier usually means</th></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Conversion</td><td>15 to 18%</td><td>Low at one site: MSA coverage or coaching. Low everywhere: pricing, weather, or training.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">MVP penetration</td><td>25 to 35%</td><td>Low: MVP not presented first, or interior lane quality.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Churn</td><td>7 to 9%; voluntary 3% or less, card 4% or less</td><td>Card churn high: failed payments not being called. Voluntary high: read the Notes reasons.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Labor</td><td>Within budget with peak coverage</td><td>High with low conversion: schedule is not matching traffic.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Audit and camera misses</td><td>Zero late opens, test car and clean every day</td><td>Repeated misses: leadership gap on that shift.</td></tr></table></div>\n<h2>Site problem or regional problem</h2>\n<p>One site off: coach the GM. Most sites off together: look for the shared cause before anyone gets coached. Weather, a price change, a POS issue, a training gap. Fix the cause once.</p>\n<h2>Spend the week on the worst gap</h2>\n<p>Rank the sites on the standard furthest from target. That is where the next visit goes. The comfortable visit to the best site can wait.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"Card churn is high at one site and normal elsewhere. Most likely?","o":["Pricing","Failed payments are not being called at that site","Weather","The POS"],"a":1,"why":"Credit card churn is recovered by calling the same week. One site high means that site is not calling."},{"q":"Conversion dropped at five of seven sites the same week. First move?","o":["Coach five GMs","Look for the shared cause before coaching anyone","Change the target","Visit the best site"],"a":1,"why":"Most sites off together is a regional cause. Fix it once."},{"q":"Where does the next site visit go?","o":["The best site","The site furthest from standard","The closest site","In rotation"],"a":1,"why":"Spend the scarcest resource where the standard is slipping."}]},{"key":"r7","title":"The GM Quarterly Meeting","minutes":4,"why":"The GM Standards set the quarterly review and forward planning with the VP of Customer Experience and Field Leadership. For the RM it is the one time each quarter the whole region's results, people, and plans are on one table. A quarter's worth of daily checks and weekly visits should make the preparation easy; if it is hard, the rhythm slipped.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Arrive with a scorecard per GM, a plan per site, and a view of the region.</p>\n<h2>Per GM</h2>\n<ul><li>Results against every standard: conversion, MVP, churn, labor, readiness, audits, administration.</li><li>Bonus standing per the GM bonus plan, and whether the GM is in good standing on the standards.</li><li>Coaching given, commitments kept, any improvement plan and its status.</li><li>Road Map standing: ready for more, on track, or needs support.</li></ul>\n<h2>Per site</h2>\n<ul><li>The one standard furthest from target and the plan to close it next quarter.</li><li>Maintenance items open more than 30 days.</li><li>Bench: the next GM and the next AGM.</li></ul>\n<h2>The region</h2>\n<ul><li>Shared causes you found and fixed, and the ones you need the VP's help with.</li><li>What you will do differently next quarter in your own rhythm.</li></ul>\n<p>Forward planning means commitments with dates, not a review of the past. Leave with yours written down.</p>","kind":"quiz","checks":[],"quiz":[{"q":"Who leads the quarterly review?","o":["The RM","The VP of Customer Experience and Field Leadership","Corporate HR","Each GM"],"a":1,"why":"Quarterly performance review and forward planning at the GM Quarterly Meeting, by the VP."},{"q":"What goes on each GM's scorecard?","o":["Conversion only","Results against every standard, bonus standing, coaching and commitments, Road Map standing","Attendance","Site photos"],"a":1,"why":"Every standard, because the bonus and the promotion decisions rest on all of them."},{"q":"If quarterly preparation is hard, what does that tell you?","o":["The quarter was busy","The daily and weekly rhythm slipped","The VP expects too much","Nothing"],"a":1,"why":"Daily checks and weekly visits should make the quarterly scorecard a summary, not a project."}]}]}],"scenarios":[{"t":"Three days under the floor","s":"One site has posted 9%, 8%, and 9% conversion on three consecutive days. The GM has not called you.","o":["Wait for the weekly visit.","The standard requires an RM review on day one. Call today: the standard, the gap, the GM's cause and plan, your check date. Log it; the pattern plus the silence matters.","Email the GM a reminder of the target.","Send an MSA from another site."],"a":1,"why":"Any day under 10% triggers an RM review. Three days and no call is two misses: the number and the ownership."},{"t":"The clean at the top site","s":"Your best site by conversion skipped the nightly clean twice this week, visible on camera. The GM is your strongest.","o":["Let it go; the numbers are great.","The same coaching conversation any GM would get, this week. Even enforcement is what makes the standard real across the region.","Mention it at the quarterly meeting.","Have the AGM handle it."],"a":1,"why":"The region learns the real standard from who gets corrected. Your top GM gets the same conversation as your newest."},{"t":"Friend of the family","s":"A GM with repeated late opens after two coaching conversations is someone you have known for years. You are tempted to give it one more quarter.","o":["One more quarter; he will turn it around.","Repeated gaps after coaching get a documented improvement plan. Write it, with support and dates, and share it with the VP. That is what the standard says and what every other GM would get.","Transfer him to a quieter site.","Say nothing until the quarterly meeting."],"a":1,"why":"\"I'll let it go this once\" is the moment the standard resets, for RMs too."},{"t":"The AGM who is ready","s":"An AGM has run her site to standard on every GM day off for six months and finished the GM level in the Academy. Her GM says she is \"not quite ready\" but cannot say what is missing.","o":["Defer to the GM.","Ask the GM for the specific standard she has not met. If there is none, she is ready, and the \"where you stand\" conversation needs to say so with a plan and a date.","Wait for an opening.","Move her to another site as AGM."],"a":1,"why":"Promote on standards held. \"Not quite ready\" without a specific gap is not an answer the Road Map allows."},{"t":"Region-wide card churn","s":"Credit card churn rose above 4% at five of seven sites this month.","o":["Coach five GMs on retention.","Find the shared cause first: a POS or billing change, a processor issue, or a region-wide gap in calling failed cards. Fix the cause once, then check each site's call list.","Lower the churn target.","Wait a month."],"a":1,"why":"Most sites off together is a regional cause. Fix it once before coaching anyone."}],"assessment":[{"q":"The RM's result is ...","o":["the best site","the region, every site to standard","visit count","audit scores"],"a":1},{"q":"Each morning the RM verifies by camera ...","o":["Sales Booth staffing","on-time open, test car, nightly clean","MSA pitches","labor"],"a":1},{"q":"A day under 10% conversion triggers ...","o":["a note","an RM review and a conversation with leadership","nothing","a new MSA"],"a":1},{"q":"A site visit starts ...","o":["in the office","as a customer","with the audit","in the tunnel"],"a":1},{"q":"A visit ends with ...","o":["ten tasks","one to three written commitments with dates","a handshake","nothing"],"a":1},{"q":"In coaching, the cause is named by ...","o":["the RM","the GM","the VP","the AGM"],"a":1},{"q":"Repeated gaps after coaching get ...","o":["one more quarter","a documented improvement plan","a transfer","silence"],"a":1},{"q":"Promotion is based on ...","o":["tenure","standards consistently met and exceeded","asking","conversion only"],"a":1},{"q":"Five sites dip together. First you ...","o":["coach five GMs","look for the shared cause","change targets","visit the best site"],"a":1},{"q":"The next visit goes to ...","o":["the best site","the site furthest from standard","the nearest","rotation"],"a":1},{"q":"The quarterly review is led by ...","o":["the RM","the VP of Customer Experience and Field Leadership","HR","the GMs"],"a":1},{"q":"Even enforcement across GMs matters because ...","o":["it is polite","the region's real standard becomes whatever the most lenient treatment allows","HR requires it","it fills the agenda"],"a":1}]}]}$academy$::jsonb);

